prop_auth_expired_2026_08_02	auth-expired-returns-invalid-key-code-no-recovery-2026-08-02	Fix auth error codes: expired/revoked keys return auth.invalid_key instead of auth.expired — agents cannot distinguish key renewal from attack, no recovery URL provided	onboarding_first_mile	8	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"verified_at": "2026-08-02T12:30:00Z", "agent_impact": "Autonomous agents cannot distinguish expired key (needs renewal) from invalid key (needs investigation). Agent with expired free-tier key loops on auth.invalid_key instead of calling POST /v1/keys/generate.", "bug_location": "src/auth.ts:345", "free_key_expiry": "Self-service keys expire in 30 days with no notification. Every agent using free key WILL hit this at day 30.", "no_recovery_url": "No auth failure includes recovery_url. Agent has no programmatic way to discover renewal path.", "spec_advertises": "OpenAPI spec and discovery.ts both advertise auth.expired as valid code but auth.ts never emits it. AUTH_EXPIRED defined in ErrorCode enum but unused in handler.", "current_behavior": "All invalid/expired/revoked keys return code auth.invalid_key. Detail text changes but machine-readable code is always auth.invalid_key.", "production_commit": "927a30b"}	Medium-high. Every free-tier agent breaks silently at 30-day key expiry and cannot self-recover. Enterprise customers cannot build key-rotation monitoring. Generates undifferentiated support tickets and churn at volume.	["Expired keys return code auth.expired not auth.invalid_key", "Revoked keys return distinct code or reason field", "Auth failures include recovery_url pointing to POST /v1/keys/generate", "Integration test verifies auth.expired emitted for expired keys", "OpenAPI Problem schema documents recovery_url"]	Fix auth error code mismatch: emit auth.expired for expired keys and add recovery_url to all auth failures	## Problem\\n\\nsrc/auth.ts:345 emits AUTH_INVALID_KEY (auth.invalid_key) for ALL auth failures - expired, revoked, invalid. AUTH_EXPIRED (auth.expired) is defined in ErrorCode enum and advertised in OpenAPI spec but never emitted.\\n\\nNo auth failure includes recovery URL. Agent whose free-tier key expired at day 30 gets 401 auth.invalid_key with no hint that POST /v1/keys/generate can renew.\\n\\n## Fix\\n\\n1. src/auth.ts ~340-348: branch on validation.status:\\n   - expired -> AUTH_EXPIRED, title API key expired\\n   - revoked -> AUTH_INVALID_KEY with reason revoked\\n   - default -> AUTH_INVALID_KEY\\n\\n2. Add recovery_url to all 401 auth failures pointing to https://www.parsethis.ai/v1/keys/generate\\n\\n3. Update problem-response.ts to support optional recovery_url\\n\\n4. Update OpenAPI Problem schema to document recovery_url\\n\\n5. Integration test: generate key, expire, call /v1/parse, assert auth.expired + recovery_url\\n\\n## Safety gates\\n\\n- Do NOT change HTTP status (stays 401)\\n- Do NOT leak key existence to unauthenticated callers\\n- Do NOT add auto-renewal\\n- Run typecheck build test before deploy	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-08-02 00:21:26.227	2026-08-02 00:21:26.227
cmsbd9xe20000ql1edhqr3w9u	docs-apex-url-breaks-every-code-example-2026-08-02	Deploy apex→www docs fix sitting uncommitted — every curl example in /docs/quickstart and /docs/api returns 405 on production	onboarding_first_mile	9	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"impact": "Every developer who copy-pastes any curl example from /docs/quickstart or /docs/api gets a 405 on their first API call. This is the first-mile conversion killer.", "deployed_commit": "927a30b139f3aa3575d5a057b3b0f7f412c2daa4", "local_fix_status": {"deployed": false, "committed": false, "fix_written": true, "diff_summary": "81 insertions, 111 deletions across 3 doc files — apex→www in all code examples", "files_changed": ["content/docs/quickstart.md", "content/docs/api.md", "content/docs/x402.md"]}, "production_findings": {"www_keygen_201": "POST https://www.parsethis.ai/v1/keys/generate → 201 Created", "apex_keygen_405": "POST https://parsethis.ai/v1/keys/generate → 405 Method Not Allowed", "api_docs_apex_urls": 12, "quickstart_apex_urls": 5, "rendered_markdown_apex": "curl -s parsethis.ai/skill, POST https://parsethis.ai/v1/keys/generate, etc."}}	Every developer who copy-pastes from the official quickstart hits a 405 on their first API call. The fix is already written locally — it just needs commit + deploy.	["Production /docs/quickstart renders zero apex (non-www) parsethis.ai URLs in curl examples", "Production /docs/api renders zero apex (non-www) parsethis.ai URLs in curl examples", "POST https://parsethis.ai/v1/keys/generate no longer returns 405 (or apex redirects to www)", "Copy-pasting any curl example from quickstart produces a successful response (201 or 200)", "git diff content/docs/ is clean after commit (no uncommitted doc changes remain)"]	Commit and deploy apex→www docs fix (content/docs/quickstart.md, api.md, x402.md)	## Problem\n\nProduction docs (deployed commit 927a30b) contain apex `parsethis.ai` URLs in every curl code example. The apex hostname returns HTTP 405 Method Not Allowed on POST endpoints (keygen, parse, policy) while `www.parsethis.ai` returns 201/200.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- `POST https://parsethis.ai/v1/keys/generate` → 405 Method Not Allowed\n- `POST https://www.parsethis.ai/v1/keys/generate` → 201 Created\n- 5 apex URLs in rendered /docs/quickstart\n- 12 apex URLs in rendered /docs/api\n- Raw markdown via Accept: text/markdown also returns apex URLs\n\n## Fix Already Written\n\nThe local working tree (HEAD: 804215b) contains the fix across 3 files:\n- content/docs/quickstart.md (apex→www in all examples)\n- content/docs/api.md (apex→www in all examples)\n- content/docs/x402.md (apex→www)\n\n81 insertions, 111 deletions. Uncommitted and undeployed.\n\n## Safety Gates\n\n1. Review the uncommitted diff for correctness before committing\n2. Run `npm run typecheck && npm run build` before deploying\n3. After deploy, verify /version shows the new commit\n4. Run production smokes: `curl -s -X POST https://www.parsethis.ai/v1/keys/generate -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{"name":"smoke"}'` → expect 201\n5. Verify rendered /docs/quickstart contains zero apex URLs via: `curl -s https://www.parsethis.ai/docs/quickstart | grep -o 'https\\?://parsethis.ai' | grep -v www | wc -l` → expect 0\n\n## Scope\n\nThis task is docs-only. No code changes, no billing/security/policy changes. Commit, push, deploy.	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-08-02 05:35:41.642	2026-08-02 05:35:41.642
cmsbingr80000u11eqtjrc2t6	keygen-abuse-ip-only-ratelimit-no-second-layer-2026-08-02	Keygen abuse vector: IP-only rate limiting trivially bypassed via rotation — add second-layer friction (email verify or CAPTCHA or fingerprint)	saas_readiness	9	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"proof": "Created 4 live API keys from a single IP in under 2 minutes with zero friction beyond the name field. Rate limit only triggered at count > 5 per minute per IP — trivially bypassed by IP rotation. Each key: pfa_live_*, 30-day expiry, scopes [analyze, evaluate, chat].", "finding": "POST /v1/keys/generate enforces only Redis-based IP rate limiting (5 keys/min/IP) and a global key cap. No email verification, no CAPTCHA, no device fingerprinting, no account binding. An attacker rotating IPs (proxies, VPNs, cloud instances) can create unlimited free keys, each granting 30 days of LLM-backed API access (analyze, evaluate, chat scopes) that costs Parse real compute via OpenRouter.", "probe_date": "2026-08-02", "cost_impact": "Each /v1/parse call invokes regex + LLM analysis + optional sandbox. Free keys with analyze/evaluate/chat scopes can issue billable-to-Parse LLM calls. At scale, this is a direct cost-bleed vector.", "current_defenses": ["Redis IP rate limit: 5 keys/min per IP (bypassable via proxy/VPN rotation)", "Global self-service key cap (configurable via SELF_SERVICE_KEY_CAP env)", "30-day expiry on free keys", "KEY_GENERATION_ENABLED toggle"], "missing_defenses": ["No email verification before key issuance", "No CAPTCHA / proof-of-work", "No device or browser fingerprinting", "No account/user binding at keygen (keys are anonymous, userId: 'self-service')", "No per-scope abuse monitoring / anomaly detection on free keys", "No cost ceiling per free key (OpenRouter calls are unbounded within rate limits)"], "affected_endpoints": ["POST /v1/keys/generate", "POST /v1/parse", "POST /v1/evaluate", "POST /v1/chat"]}	Closes a direct cost-bleed and resource-exhaustion vector before customer volume. Without second-layer friction, a single attacker with a proxy pool can create thousands of free keys, each consuming LLM compute at Parse's expense for 30 days. Also blocks global key cap exhaustion that would lock out legitimate users.	["Email verification OR CAPTCHA (e.g., hCaptcha/Turnstile) required before keygen issues a key", "If email verification: key is created in 'pending' state, activated only after verification link click", "Rate limit remains IP-based as first layer, second layer is email/fingerprint/CAPTCHA", "Free key has a per-key monthly request cap (e.g., 100 requests) enforced at the API gateway, not just per-minute rate limit", "Anomaly detection: >10 keys from same email domain or fingerprint triggers admin alert", "Existing self-service keygen flow for skill-based auto-provisioning (agents without email) still works via a fallback path (e.g., x402-only or time-limited trial key)", "Load test: 100 keygen attempts from 100 different IPs without email/CAPTCHA yields ≤5 active keys"]	Add second-layer keygen friction: email verification or CAPTCHA + per-key request cap	## Context\nPOST /v1/keys/generate currently has only IP-based Redis rate limiting (5/min/IP) and a global cap. No email verification, CAPTCHA, fingerprinting, or account binding. Trivially bypassed via IP rotation.\n\n## Implementation\n1. Add optional email field to keygen request body. If provided, send verification link; key created in 'pending' state until verified.\n2. If no email (agent/skill auto-provisioning path): issue a time-limited trial key (24h instead of 30d) with lower rate limits and a hard request cap (e.g., 20 requests). Upgrade to full free key after email verification or first x402 payment.\n3. Add per-free-key monthly request cap (configurable, e.g., 100 req/month) enforced at screening/evaluate/chat route level, not just per-minute rate limit.\n4. Add anomaly detection: >N keys from same email domain or fingerprint within 24h triggers admin alert via audit_event.\n5. Consider Cloudflare Turnstile (free, privacy-respecting CAPTCHA alternative) for the /pricing page keygen button.\n\n## Safety gates\n- Do NOT break the existing skill-based auto-provisioning flow (agents that POST /v1/keys/generate without email). Trial key fallback must work.\n- Do NOT change billing/security policy. Per-key cap is a rate limit, not a paid feature gate.\n- Do NOT store CAPTCHA tokens or email addresses in plaintext beyond verification window.\n- Test: skill install + keygen still works, /v1/parse still works with verified key, trial key expires correctly, per-key cap blocks after N requests.\n\n## Verification\n- [ ] Unverified/trial key has 24h expiry and 20-request cap\n- [ ] Email-verified key has 30-day expiry and 100-request/month cap\n- [ ] IP rotation attack (100 IPs) yields ≤5 verified keys\n- [ ] Skill auto-provisioning still works without email\n- [ ] Per-key cap returns 429 with clear upgrade-to-Pro messaging	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-08-02 08:06:11.348	2026-08-02 08:06:11.348
cmsbl9a6y00005d1e29fsfpf3	key-self-introspection-get-endpoint-2026-08-02	Add GET /v1/keys/self — agents that self-provision keys cannot check their own status, expiry, scopes, tier, or remaining quota	onboarding	8	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"finding": "DELETE /v1/keys/self exists but GET /v1/keys/self returns 404. Self-provisioned keys (POST /v1/keys/generate) receive their metadata exactly once at creation, then have no introspection endpoint. Agents cannot verify if their key is still valid, check when it expires, see what scopes they have, or read their rate-limit tier.", "agent_impact": "Agents following the /skill prompt auto-generate keys and store them. But they cannot pre-flight: is this key still valid? Am I about to hit rate limit? When does it expire? They discover expiry only when /v1/parse starts returning auth errors — by which point the agent is broken with no recovery path.", "verified_failures": ["GET /v1/keys/self with valid bearer -> 404 Not Found", "GET /v1/keys/self/usage with valid bearer -> 404 Not Found", "GET /v1/usage with valid bearer -> 404 Not Found"], "related_but_distinct": ["free-key-30day-silent-expiry-no-renewal-path-2026-08-01: addresses the expiry/renewal policy, not the introspection endpoint", "auth-expired-returns-invalid-key-code-no-recovery-2026-08-02: addresses error codes for expired keys, not proactive status checking", "dashboard-no-account-usage-management-2026-07-31: addresses human dashboard UI, not agent-facing API endpoint"], "keygen_response_shape": "POST /v1/keys/generate returns id, key (one-time), scopes [analyze, evaluate, chat], expires_at (+30 days), note. After this response, the key metadata is unreachable.", "route_exists_but_incomplete": "src/routes/keys.ts line 39: keysRoutes.delete(\\"/v1/keys/self\\", ...) — only DELETE handler, no GET counterpart"}	Agents gain self-diagnostics: check key validity, expiry countdown, scope inventory, and rate-limit tier before making calls. Enables graceful degradation (regenerate before expiry, warn before rate limit) and pre-flight health checks. This is the minimum API surface for agents that manage their own lifecycle.	["GET /v1/keys/self with valid bearer returns 200 with: id, name, scopes, tier, rate_limit, created_at, expires_at, last_used_at, revoked_at", "GET /v1/keys/self without bearer returns 401", "GET /v1/keys/self with revoked/expired key returns appropriate error code (not generic invalid_key)", "Response never includes the full key string or key hash", "Rate-limited key still returns metadata (not 429) so agents can read their own status while throttled", "OpenAPI spec includes GET /v1/keys/self", "/skill prompt updated to mention self-introspection for pre-flight checks", "Optional: GET /v1/keys/self/usage returns current billing period request count and remaining quota"]	Add GET /v1/keys/self endpoint for key introspection (scopes, tier, expiry, rate limit)	SCOPE: Add a GET handler at /v1/keys/self in src/routes/keys.ts alongside the existing DELETE handler. Use authMiddleware() to resolve the calling key. Return non-secret metadata: id, name, scopes, tier, rate_limit, created_at, expires_at, last_used_at, revoked_at. Never return the full key or hash. Add to OpenAPI spec. Update /skill prompt to mention self-introspection for pre-flight checks. Optionally add GET /v1/keys/self/usage for billing-period request count.\n\nSAFETY GATES: No changes to auth logic, rate limiting logic, key generation, billing, or security policy. Read-only endpoint only. No secrets returned. Test with npm run typecheck && npm run build && npm run test.\n\nVERIFICATION: GET /v1/keys/self with fresh self-service key returns 200 + metadata. No bearer -> 401. Expired key -> appropriate error. Rate-limited key -> still 200. Full key string not in response.	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Master Key:master	Master Key:master	2026-08-02 09:19:08.506	2026-08-02 09:19:08.506
cmsbnryqy0000mh1ervhu2du1	elon-saas-legal-pages-missing-terms-aup-2026-08-02	Add Terms of Service and Acceptable Use Policy pages — hard legal blocker for SaaS	docs-trust-legal	9	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"aup_status": {"url": "https://www.parsethis.ai/aup", "http_status": 404}, "probe_date": "2026-08-02", "tos_variant": {"url": "https://www.parsethis.ai/tos", "http_status": 404}, "pricing_page": "has subscribe CTAs but no Terms/AUP reference for customers to accept before paying", "terms_status": {"url": "https://www.parsethis.ai/terms", "http_status": 404}, "privacy_working": {"url": "https://www.parsethis.ai/privacy", "http_status": 200}, "footer_legal_links": "only /privacy present; no /terms or /aup in footer", "stripe_integration": "billing routes exist; checkout will collect payments without customers accepting any ToS", "acceptable_use_variant": {"url": "https://www.parsethis.ai/acceptable-use", "http_status": 404}}	A SaaS product that charges customers without Terms of Service or Acceptable Use Policy has no contractual basis, no usage rules enforcement basis, and creates liability exposure. Enterprise/security customers will not sign without these. Stripe may require ToS for compliance. Cheapest highest-impact fix remaining for SaaS readiness.	["/terms returns HTTP 200 with real Terms of Service content", "/aup returns HTTP 200 with AUP content", "Both pages linked in footer alongside /privacy on all pages", "/pricing page references Terms of Service before checkout CTA"]	Create Terms of Service and AUP pages with footer + checkout integration	Add /terms and /aup as static content pages (same pattern as /privacy). Write content specific to Parse as a prompt-security API: service scope, acceptable use, payment/refund policy, liability, key management responsibilities. Add footer links to all page templates. Reference ToS on /pricing page before checkout CTAs.\n\nSafety gates: Do NOT change existing /privacy content. Do NOT change billing/Stripe logic. Do NOT collect new personal data. Legal copy should be reviewed by Danny before publish.	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Master Key:master	Master Key:master	2026-08-02 10:29:39.37	2026-08-02 10:29:39.37
cmsbqc3020000o41es7pcyscu	hourly-execlimit-429-no-upgrade-url-2026-08-02	Hourly execution rate-limit 429 — the FIRST wall free users hit — has zero upgrade/conversion path (daily and monthly caps do)	conversion_friction	7	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"finding": "Three rate-limit surfaces exist in parse.ts. Only the FIRST one a free user hits lacks any upgrade guidance.", "surfaces": {"daily_cost_cap": {"detail": "Daily execution cost cap reached", "location": "src/routes/parse.ts ~line 427", "hits_first": false, "header_name": "X-Upgrade-URL", "http_status": 429, "free_tier_limit": "$0.50/day (~100 calls)", "has_upgrade_url": false, "has_upgrade_header": true}, "hourly_exec_limit": {"note": "At $0.005/parse, a free user hits this in ~5 minutes of testing. This is the highest-frequency rejection surface and it's a dead end.", "detail": "Execution rate limit exceeded", "location": "src/routes/parse.ts ~line 413", "hits_first": true, "http_status": 429, "free_tier_limit": "5 requests/hour", "has_upgrade_url": false, "has_upgrade_header": false}, "monthly_usage_cap": {"note": "Only applies to PAID tiers — free tier skips billableUsageMiddleware entirely (early return at line 16)", "detail": "Monthly request cap exceeded", "location": "src/lib/billable-usage-middleware.ts ~line 42", "hits_first": false, "http_status": 429, "has_upgrade_url": true, "has_upgrade_header": true}}, "probe_date": "2026-08-02T11:33Z", "agent_impact": "Autonomous agents hitting the hourly 429 see retryable:true and Retry-After:3600 but no upgradeUrl field. Agent cannot surface upgrade option to operator — it just backs off or gives up. Discovery docs (discovery.ts line 519) already promise upgradeUrl on cap responses, but the hourly limit violates that contract.", "production_commit": "927a30b"}	Every free-tier user or agent testing Parse hits this wall within minutes. Current response is a dead-end 429 with Retry-After:3600 — no upgrade path, no pricing link, no way to distinguish 'try again later' from 'you need to upgrade'. Adding upgradeUrl + X-Upgrade-URL header (matching the pattern already used by the daily/monthly caps) converts the highest-frequency rejection into a conversion touchpoint. Agents can surface the upgrade option; humans get a clickable path. Discovery API contract already documents this field on other 429s — this fixes a consistency gap.	["Hourly exec limit 429 response body includes upgradeUrl: '/pricing' field", "Hourly exec limit 429 response includes X-Upgrade-URL: /pricing header", "observe-mode rate limit 429 (parse.ts ~line 387) also includes upgradeUrl + X-Upgrade-URL (same gap)", "All three rate-limit surfaces return consistent fields: code, retryable, limit, window, upgradeUrl", "Discovery OpenAPI error schema matches actual response (discovery.ts already documents upgradeUrl — make hourly limit conform)", "Add regression test asserting upgradeUrl present on hourly 429 response", "No behavior change to actual rate-limit thresholds — only response metadata"]	Add upgradeUrl + X-Upgrade-URL to hourly execution rate-limit 429 responses	## Context\n\nThe hourly execution rate limit in parse.ts (~line 413) returns a bare 429 with no upgrade path. This is the FIRST rejection surface free users encounter (5 requests/hour). The daily cost cap and monthly usage cap both include upgrade guidance — the hourly limit is the outlier.\n\n## What to change\n\n1. In `src/routes/parse.ts`, the hourly exec limit 429 block (~line 410-420):\n   - Add `c.header("X-Upgrade-URL", "/pricing");`\n   - Add `upgradeUrl: "/pricing"` to the problem() body\n\n2. In the observe-mode rate limit 429 (~line 385-395), add the same fields.\n\n3. Add regression test in parse route tests asserting `upgradeUrl` field present on hourly limit 429.\n\n## Safety gates\n- DO NOT change rate-limit thresholds or enforcement behavior\n- DO NOT change the free-tier skip in billableUsageMiddleware\n- Only add response metadata fields — no logic changes\n- Verify discovery.ts OpenAPI schema already documents upgradeUrl (it does) — ensure conformance\n- Run `npm run typecheck && npm run build && npm test` before deploy\n\n## Files\n- src/routes/parse.ts (2 blocks: hourly exec limit + observe-mode limit)\n- Test file for rate-limit response assertions	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-08-02 11:41:17.234	2026-08-02 11:41:17.234
cmsbsrlss0000x31egqszarwk	auth-db-timeout-resilience-redis-cache-2026-08-02	Auth key validation returns 503 on intermittent DB timeouts — Redis cache fallback not masking DB flakiness for customer API calls	reliability	9	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"impact_at_volume": "At customer volume, even a 1% DB timeout rate means 1 in 100 API calls fail with 503. For an API-security product where customers integrate into production pipelines, intermittent 503s on auth are catastrophic — the agent or pipeline fails, the customer loses trust, and the security gate becomes a reliability liability rather than an asset.", "master_key_works": "MASTER_API_KEY bypass (auth.ts line 216) uses timing-safe comparison against env var, never touches DB. This proves the auth path can work without DB — the cache just needs to serve validated keys the same way.", "live_reproduction": "During this hourly loop run (2026-08-02T12:44Z), admin action calls via local Node runtime hit 503 'Authentication service unavailable / API key validation is temporarily unavailable' twice before succeeding on retry. The DB was reachable for some queries (dashboard snapshot succeeded) while auth validation timed out.", "failure_mode_in_code": "src/api-key-service.ts line 318: when DB lookup times out, returns {status: 'temporarily_unavailable', reason: 'db_lookup_timeout'} which propagates as 503 to the caller. src/auth.ts line 216: master key bypasses DB entirely, but regular customer keys have no such bypass.", "audit_events_db_timeout": [{"action": "auth_failure", "detail": "No API key provided", "created_at": "2026-08-01T14:59:11Z"}, {"action": "auth_failure", "detail": "API key validation temporarily unavailable: db_lookup_timeout", "created_at": "2026-07-26T18:09:33Z"}, {"action": "auth_failure", "detail": "API key validation temporarily unavailable: db_lookup_timeout", "created_at": "2026-07-26T18:06:27Z"}], "redis_cache_present_but_insufficient": "api-key-service.ts has cache_lookup_failed and fallback_lookup_failed paths, indicating a Redis cache layer exists. But the cache is not effectively serving during DB outages — the db_lookup_timeout still reaches the customer as 503."}	Eliminates the single highest-trust-erosion failure mode for customer API calls. Makes auth resilient to Postgres flakiness — which is the most common Railway-managed-DB issue. Without this fix, any real customer using Parse in production will experience random 503s on their security screening calls, making the product less reliable than not having security screening at all.	["When DATABASE_URL is temporarily unreachable, authenticated API calls using a previously-validated key still succeed (serve from Redis cache) instead of returning 503", "Redis cache TTL for validated API keys is at least 60 seconds (enough to mask transient DB blips without serving revoked keys too long)", "Key revocation invalidates the Redis cache entry within 5 seconds (admin.api_key.revoke calls cache invalidation)", "A synthetic test simulating DB unavailability (mock Prisma timeout) verifies that a cached key still authenticates successfully", "The db_lookup_timeout path in api-key-service.ts falls back to Redis cache before returning temporarily_unavailable", "Admin dashboard shows cache hit rate metric for API key validation (observability into whether the cache is working)", "No new secrets, no production config changes, no billing impact — pure reliability hardening"]	Harden auth key validation: Redis cache fallback must serve validated keys during DB timeouts instead of returning 503	## Problem\nAPI key validation (src/api-key-service.ts, src/auth.ts) returns 503 when the Postgres DB times out. This is intermittent — the DB is generally up but auth lookups randomly fail. 3 db_lookup_timeout audit events logged since 7/26. Reproduced live during this loop run.\n\n## Root Cause\nThe Redis cache layer exists but does not serve as a fallback when the DB lookup times out. The code path is: cache miss → DB lookup → timeout → return temporarily_unavailable → 503 to customer. It should be: cache miss → DB lookup → timeout → serve stale cache entry (if exists) or fail gracefully with a longer-lived cache.\n\n## Scope\n1. In api-key-service.ts: when db_lookup_timeout occurs, check Redis for a previously-cached key validation result before returning temporarily_unavailable. Serve the cached result if available.\n2. Set Redis cache TTL for validated keys to 60s minimum.\n3. Ensure admin.api_key.revoke invalidates the cache entry immediately (so revoked keys don't linger).\n4. Add a synthetic test: mock DB timeout, verify cached key still authenticates.\n5. Add cache hit/miss counter to admin dashboard for observability.\n\n## Safety Gates\n- Do NOT change the master key bypass path (already works correctly)\n- Do NOT increase cache TTL beyond 60s (revocation latency)\n- Do NOT remove the 503 error path entirely — if both DB and cache fail, still return 503 (fail-closed is correct for a security product)\n- Test with revoked keys to ensure cache invalidation works\n- No production config changes, no secrets, no billing impact	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Master Key:master	Master Key:master	2026-08-02 12:49:20.668	2026-08-02 12:49:20.668
cmsbvebpx00006d1etrvph4eb	elon-dashboard-keygen-orphan-not-linked-from-any-cta-2026-08-02	Route all keygen CTAs to /dashboard (the only working key-gen UI) — homepage, pricing, playground, and docs all send users to dead-end paths	conversion_friction	8	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"probed_at": "2026-08-02T22:00Z", "pricing_cta": "Pricing 'Generate Free Key' -> GET /v1/keys/generate (POST-only endpoint, returns 404)", "homepage_cta": "Homepage 'Get API key' -> /docs/quickstart (no keygen UI, just curl docs)", "keygen_prefix": "pfa_live", "dashboard_keygen": "/dashboard has working JS button: POST /v1/keys/generate -> 201 with key", "docs_curl_domain": "Quickstart curl uses parsethis.ai (apex) — tested: connection timeout (000); www.parsethis.ai/v1/keys/generate returns 201", "dashboard_orphaned": "No page on parsethis.ai links to /dashboard — it is unreachable via navigation", "total_api_keys_in_db": 0, "keygen_scopes_returned": ["analyze", "evaluate", "chat"]}	Primary free-tier conversion funnel is leaking at every CTA. Homepage 'Get API key' links to /docs/quickstart whose curl commands use the apex domain (parsethis.ai) which times out — the actual working keygen is POST www.parsethis.ai/v1/keys/generate (201). /dashboard has a working 'Generate API Key' button calling /v1/keys/generate via JS — but no page on the site links to /dashboard. /pricing 'Generate Free Key' is a GET anchor to a POST-only endpoint (404). Result: a motivated visitor following the primary CTA hits a timeout or 404, not a key.	["Homepage 'Get API key' CTA links to /dashboard", "Pricing page keygen CTA links to /dashboard (no GET on POST-only endpoint)", "Playground 'Get API key' CTA links to /dashboard", "/dashboard is discoverable from primary navigation (nav bar or footer)", "/docs/quickstart curl examples use www.parsethis.ai (not bare apex)", "Fresh incognito browser session: homepage -> CTA -> /dashboard -> Generate Key -> key displayed with copy button"]	Route all keygen CTAs to /dashboard and fix docs curl domain to www	CHANGES:\n1. Homepage: change 'Get API key' href from /docs/quickstart to /dashboard\n2. Pricing: change 'Generate Free Key' to link to /dashboard (remove GET anchor to POST-only /v1/keys/generate)\n3. Playground: change 'Get API key' CTA to link to /dashboard\n4. Navigation: add /dashboard to primary nav bar\n5. Docs: update /docs/quickstart curl examples — parsethis.ai → www.parsethis.ai\n6. Post-keygen: add quickstart 'first API call' link to /dashboard key-gen success state\n\nSAFETY GATES:\n- No changes to billing, Stripe, security policy, or auth logic\n- No secrets or full API keys in any UI\n- /dashboard keygen JS already works (confirmed: 201 response) — only navigation/href changes\n- Test all CTAs return working keygen after change\n- Verify with fresh incognito browser session\n- typecheck + build before deploy	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	2026-08-02 14:02:59.925	2026-08-02 14:02:59.925
prop_railway_auth_2026_08_02	railway-auth-restoration-unblocks-all-proposals-2026-08-02	P0 BLOCKER: Railway CLI unauthorized — 42 proposals invisible in production /admin, revenue path blocked, 0 fixes shipped	ops	1	proposed	high	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"github_push": "working (ls-remote succeeded)", "local_repo_state": "4 commits behind origin/main, 79 modified + 25 untracked files (mostly blog content + temp scripts)", "production_commit": "927a30b (latest origin/main — production IS current)", "local_db_proposals": 42, "railway_cli_status": "Unauthorized. Please run railway login again.", "auto_deploy_unverified": "Railway railway.toml uses Dockerfile builder but auto-deploy trigger status unverifiable without CLI", "signup_checkout_status": "429 — self-service key cap hit, revenue conversion blocked", "master_key_local_vs_prod": "local MASTER_API_KEY returns 401 against production /v1/admin/actions", "production_db_proposals_visible": "unknown — cannot verify without production admin API access"}	Restoring Railway auth is the single action that unblocks everything: deploy pipeline, production admin API access (to write proposals where Danny can see them), production DB inspection (to revoke orphaned self-service keys and clear the signup-checkout 429 revenue blocker), and deploy verification.	["railway whoami returns authenticated user", "railway status shows service health", "Production /v1/admin/actions accepts admin action calls", "At least 5 high-priority proposals visible and actionable in parsethis.ai/admin", "Self-service key cap can be inspected and orphaned keys revoked in production"]	Restore Railway CLI authentication	## What\n\nRun `railway login --browserless` to get an auth URL/code, complete in browser, then verify:\n1. `railway whoami` shows authenticated user\n2. `railway status` shows service health\n3. Production `/v1/admin/actions` accepts the production MASTER_API_KEY\n4. Local proposals replayed to production DB via admin API\n\n## Why\n\n42 improvement proposals filed in local DB. Zero visible in production /admin. Zero implemented. Signup-checkout returns 429 (revenue blocked). Railway CLI is the only blocked path to deploy, inspect production DB, and make proposals actionable.\n\n## Safety gates\n- `railway login` is authentication only — no production mutations\n- No env changes, no restarts, no migrations without separate explicit approval\n- Do NOT revoke production self-service keys or clear Redis fallback until Railway auth confirmed and production state verified\n- Proposal replay to production DB is append-only (INSERT proposals) — no destructive writes\n\n## Cost of inaction\nEvery hour blocked: zero revenue (signup-checkout 429), zero shipped fixes, growing invisible proposal backlog.	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-08-02 12:40:00.789	2026-08-02 12:40:00.789
cmsc3gk0u0000111e7wsxd0xv	elon-anonymous-keygen-zero-retention-loop-2026-08-02	Anonymous keygen = zero retention loop: 30-day keys with no email capture means 100% silent churn, no upsell path, no re-engagement possible	conversion_friction	10	proposed	medium	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"source": "live_production_probe", "findings": ["POST /v1/keys/generate accepts only {name: string} — no email, no account, no identity", "Generated keys expire in exactly 30 days with no renewal notification mechanism", "Dashboard shows 2 total API keys (both internal Kublai/owner) — zero external customer keys", "0 screening events, 0 evaluations, 0 x402 payments in production — no product usage follows keygen", "Keygen response includes no account/identity token, no dashboard URL for key management", "Pricing page checkout JS reads localStorage key but there is no account to link the key to", "Free tier users have no way to be contacted before expiry, after expiry, or for upgrade nudges", "Existing keygen-abuse proposal frames this as security; the bigger problem is SaaS economics: anonymous keys = unmeasurable, uncontactable, unconvertable users"], "collected_at": "2026-08-02T17:48:40.922Z", "probe_results": {"parse_smoke": "POST /v1/parse with free key — 200, risk_score 0, product works", "keygen_endpoint": "POST https://www.parsethis.ai/v1/keys/generate — 201, scopes: [analyze, evaluate, chat], no email field", "total_evaluations": 0, "total_screening_events": 0, "total_paid_subscriptions": 0}}	Without identity at keygen, Parse cannot retain, contact, upsell, or measure any free-tier user. Every free key is a one-shot interaction that silently expires. This is the structural reason the product has zero usage despite a working API. Adding optional email capture at keygen (with verification) unlocks: expiry reminders, upgrade emails, usage analytics, account-based key management, and the entire SaaS retention loop.	["POST /v1/keys/generate accepts optional email field (does not break existing anonymous keygen)", "If email provided, send verification email with confirmation link", "30 days before key expiry, send renewal reminder email to verified addresses", "Verified email users can manage/revoke/regenerate keys from /dashboard", "Admin can see email-linked keys vs anonymous keys in dashboard.snapshot", "Existing anonymous keygen continues to work without email (backward compatible)", "Email addresses stored hashed or encrypted, never returned in API responses", "Add /v1/keys/self endpoint so agents can check their own key status and expiry (existing proposal dependency)"]	Add optional email capture + verification at keygen, expiry reminders, and account-linked key management	## Objective\nAdd the minimum identity layer that makes Parse a SaaS business instead of an anonymous API.\n\n## Scope\n1. Extend POST /v1/keys/generate to accept optional {email: string}\n2. If email provided: store hashed email, send verification link, mark key email_verified=false until confirmed\n3. Add cron/worker job: 7 days before key expiry, send renewal reminder to verified emails\n4. Extend /dashboard to show email-linked keys with manage/regenerate/revoke actions\n5. Add GET /v1/keys/self for key self-introspection (overlaps existing proposal)\n\n## Safety Gates\n- Do NOT break existing anonymous keygen — email must remain optional\n- Do NOT require email for x402 pay-per-call path (those users pay per request)\n- Store emails hashed/encrypted — never return raw email in any API response\n- Rate limit verification email sends (max 3 per email per hour)\n- No payment/billing changes in this scope — pure identity + notification layer\n- GDPR: add unsubscribe link in every email, support email deletion request via admin action\n\n## Out of Scope (gated separately)\n- Password-based accounts / OAuth login (separate proposal)\n- Stripe customer creation at keygen time (only after first paid conversion)\n- Marketing email sequences (only transactional: verify, expiry, key-status)\n\n## Test Plan\n- Generate anonymous key (no email) → still works exactly as before\n- Generate key with email → verification email sent, key works pre-verification\n- Verify email → key marked email_verified, visible in admin\n- Key near expiry → reminder email sent\n- Email never returned in API responses or admin API\n- Backward compatibility: all existing keys continue to work	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-08-02 17:48:40.925	2026-08-02 17:48:40.925
cmscaqruq00009x1eezyhcsgx	elon-signup-checkout-hardcap-blocks-revenue-2026-08-02	P0 REVENUE BLOCKER: signup-checkout hard-capped at 100 self-service keys — every new paid signup gets 429, zero revenue path for cold visitors	billing	10	proposed	high	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"probe_time": "2026-08-02T21:11:00Z", "code_evidence": {"cap": 100, "file": "src/routes/billing.ts:223", "comment": "hardcoded if (totalKeys >= 100) return 429", "free_keygen_cap": "src/routes/public.ts:49 DEFAULT_SELF_SERVICE_KEY_CAP = 1_000"}, "pricing_page_js": "fetch('/v1/billing/signup-checkout',{method:'POST',body:JSON.stringify({tier:'pro'})}) → 429 for every cold visitor", "production_health": {"health": "200 ok", "payments_total": 0, "version_commit": "927a30b", "subscriptions_active": 0}, "free_keygen_response": {"status": "still working (cap=1000)", "http_status": 201}, "fallback_on_pricing_page": "mailto:d@kurult.ai (manual email, not a product path)", "signup_checkout_response": {"body": "Maximum number of self-service keys reached", "http_status": 429}}	Production /v1/billing/signup-checkout returns HTTP 429 "Maximum number of self-service keys reached" for ALL new visitors. This endpoint is the ONLY path on /pricing for a cold (unauthenticated) visitor to start a Pro ($49/mo) or Team ($199/mo) subscription. Free keygen (/v1/keys/generate) has a 1,000-key cap and still works, but the paid signup path is dead. Zero new revenue can enter through the primary conversion funnel.	["POST /v1/billing/signup-checkout with {tier:\\"pro\\"} returns 201 with checkout_url for a new visitor", "Self-service key cap for signup-checkout is raised to match free keygen cap (1000) or made configurable via SELF_SERVICE_KEY_CAP env var", "Billing endpoint cap is decoupled from free keygen cap — paid signups should never be blocked by a free-tier quota", "Pricing page \\"Start Pro\\" button produces a working Stripe checkout URL without falling back to mailto", "Verify with: curl -X POST https://www.parsethis.ai/v1/billing/signup-checkout -H \\"Content-Type: application/json\\" -d '{\\"tier\\":\\"pro\\"}' → 201"]	Fix signup-checkout 100-key hard cap blocking all paid conversions	## Problem\n/v1/billing/signup-checkout is hard-capped at 100 self-service keys (src/routes/billing.ts:223). Production has hit this cap. Every new visitor clicking "Start Pro" on /pricing gets HTTP 429.\n\n## Fix\n1. Raise the cap in billing.ts to match the free keygen cap (1000) or read from SELF_SERVICE_KEY_CAP env var (same pattern as src/routes/public.ts:144).\n2. Better: decouple entirely — signup-checkout creates a key + Stripe checkout session, so it should not share the free keygen cap at all. Consider removing the cap for signup-checkout and relying solely on the per-IP rate limit (5/min) already in place.\n3. Also clean up expired/revoked self-service keys via a cron or migration to prevent cap exhaustion from dead keys.\n\n## Safety gates\n- Do NOT change Stripe price IDs, webhook secrets, or checkout session logic\n- Do NOT remove the per-IP rate limiter (5/min) — that prevents abuse\n- Test with dry-run first: verify countSelfServiceKeys() returns < cap after cleanup\n- Verify checkout_url is a valid Stripe URL before returning 201\n- Deploy and verify via: curl -X POST https://www.parsethis.ai/v1/billing/signup-checkout -d '{"tier":"pro"}' → 201 with checkout_url\n- This is a billing-adjacent change — Danny must approve before deploy\n\n## Context\n- Free keygen (/v1/keys/generate): cap=1000, still working\n- Paid signup (/v1/billing/signup-checkout): cap=100, DEAD (429 for all visitors)\n- Production: 0 active subscriptions, 0 payments, 0 revenue\n- This is the single highest-impact revenue fix: unblocks the entire paid funnel	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Master Key:master	Master Key:master	2026-08-02 21:12:34.946	2026-08-02 21:12:34.946
cmscd34zm0000ey1eqvzpknzf	elon-apex-path-stripping-redirect-fix-2026-08-02	Apex domain strips path on www redirect — parsethis.ai/pricing, /docs/quickstart, /admin all 404 for bare-domain visitors	reliability	10	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"www_dns": ["69.46.46.119", "CNAME: 4liqu6bz.up.railway.app"], "apex_dns": ["15.197.225.128", "3.33.251.168"], "probe_time": "2026-08-02T22:16Z", "apex_probes": {"parsethis.ai": {"status": 301, "redirects_to": "https://www.parsethis.ai", "path_preserved": "no"}, "parsethis.ai/admin": {"status": 404}, "parsethis.ai/health": {"status": 404}, "parsethis.ai/pricing": {"status": 404}, "parsethis.ai/llms.txt": {"status": 404}, "parsethis.ai/docs/quickstart": {"status": 404}}, "deployed_commit": "927a30b139f3aa3575d5a057b3b0f7f412c2daa4", "impact_estimate": "Anyone typing parsethis.ai/anything or clicking a bare-domain link from a tweet, email, or blog gets a 404. The apex root works only because it redirects to www root with no path.", "redirect_header": "HTTP/2 301 Location: https://www.parsethis.ai (path dropped)", "www_probes_all_200": true, "existing_related_proposal": "docs-apex-url-breaks-every-code-example-2026-08-02 — that fixes URLs inside doc content; this fixes the DNS/redirect infrastructure itself"}	Every bare-domain link with a path is dead. A tweet saying parsethis.ai/pricing, a blog linking parsethis.ai/docs/quickstart, a customer typing parsethis.ai/admin — all 404. The apex forwarding layer (AWS Global Accelerator IPs 15.197.225.128/3.33.251.168) does a 301 to www but strips the original path. This is invisible to the www-only monitoring canaries and silently kills conversion from any non-www traffic source.	["GET parsethis.ai/pricing returns 301 to https://www.parsethis.ai/pricing (path preserved)", "GET parsethis.ai/docs/quickstart returns 301 to https://www.parsethis.ai/docs/quickstart (path preserved)", "GET parsethis.ai/admin returns 301 to https://www.parsethis.ai/admin (path preserved)", "GET parsethis.ai/health returns 301 to https://www.parsethis.ai/health (path preserved)", "Bare parsethis.ai continues to redirect to www root as before", "No impact on www.parsethis.ai — all www paths continue to work directly"]	Configure path-preserving apex→www redirect at DNS/forwarding layer	## Problem\nThe apex domain parsethis.ai uses an AWS forwarding layer (15.197.225.128, 3.33.251.168) that issues HTTP 301 to https://www.parsethis.ai but drops the request path. Result: every bare-domain URL with a path returns 404.\n\n## Evidence (probed 2026-08-02T22:16Z)\n- parsethis.ai → 301 → https://www.parsethis.ai (works, no path)\n- parsethis.ai/pricing → 404\n- parsethis.ai/docs/quickstart → 404\n- parsethis.ai/admin → 404\n- parsethis.ai/health → 404\n- parsethis.ai/llms.txt → 404\n- All corresponding www.parsethis.ai paths → 200\n\n## Root Cause\nThe apex DNS (A records: 15.197.225.128, 3.33.251.168) points to a forwarding/redirect service that strips the path on redirect. The www subdomain CNAMEs to Railway directly and works correctly.\n\n## Fix Options (in priority order)\n1. **Railway apex custom domain**: Add parsethis.ai as a Railway custom domain alongside www. Railway can serve the apex directly, eliminating the redirect layer entirely. This is the cleanest fix.\n2. **Path-preserving redirect**: Configure the apex forwarding service (likely GoDaddy DNS forwarding or AWS Global Accelerator redirect rule) to preserve the path: 301 to https://www.parsethis.ai${path} instead of stripping it.\n3. **ALB/CloudFront redirect rule**: If using an AWS layer, add a redirect rule that captures the path and appends it to the www redirect target.\n\n## Safety Gates\n- This is a DNS/infrastructure change — Danny must approve before applying\n- Test in staging or with a single path first before flipping globally\n- Verify www.parsethis.ai is completely unaffected\n- No application code changes needed\n- DNS changes have propagation delay — monitor for 48h after\n- Do NOT remove the www DNS/CNAME — only fix the apex behavior\n\n## Related\n- Existing proposal docs-apex-url-breaks-every-code-example fixes URLs inside doc content to use www. This proposal fixes the infrastructure so apex links also work. Both are needed for full coverage.	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	d@kurult.ai Team Key:ck_ea844734a98ca7a93875f26b	d@kurult.ai Team Key:ck_ea844734a98ca7a93875f26b	2026-08-02 22:18:11.073	2026-08-02 22:18:11.073
