cmsl4n46f004jjv1em0eu744m	elon-deploy-observability-zero-commit-metadata-2026-08-09	P0 META-BLOCKER: Production has zero deploy observability — RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA never baked into container, /version returns commit:unknown, all 62 proposals unmeasurable	reliability	10	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"repo_head": "a31a945", "collected_at": "2026-08-09T01:29:00Z", "runtime_defect": "Dockerfile runs RUN npx tsc (compiles to dist/) but CMD is tsx src/index.ts — runs uncompiled source, so import.meta.url is /src/ not /dist/, reporting runtime:source", "build_info_code": "src/lib/build-info.ts reads process.env.RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA and PARSE_COMMIT_SHA via firstNonEmpty() — code is correct, env var is never set in container", "dockerfile_defect": "Dockerfile does not ARG/ENV RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA — Railway Dockerfile builder injects it as build arg but Dockerfile never consumes it", "railway_toml_startCommand": "npx tsx src/index.ts (source mode, not dist)", "production_version_response": {"commit": "unknown", "runtime": "source", "build_time": "unknown"}, "impact_on_existing_proposals": "62 proposed improvement proposals have acceptance criteria like verify /version shows deploy commit — none can be met until this is fixed"}	Production cannot identify what code is running. Every deploy verification in the entire improvement backlog is structurally blocked. 62 proposals with verify /version acceptance criteria are untestable. Engineers cannot confirm a fix shipped, agents cannot verify deploy state, incident response is blind. This is the meta-bottleneck: you cannot iterate on a system you cannot observe.	["Dockerfile adds ARG RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA at top and ENV PARSE_COMMIT_SHA=$RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA", "Dockerfile adds ARG RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_CREATED_AT and ENV PARSE_BUILD_TIME=$RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_CREATED_AT", "railway.toml or Dockerfile ensures RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA is passed as build arg", "Production GET /version returns commit field with actual SHA (not unknown)", "Production GET /version returns build_time with actual timestamp (not unknown)", "If tsx is required for Prisma ESM, document why in Dockerfile comment and consider node dist/index.js with .js imports", "At least one existing P0 proposal (e.g. schema drift or Stripe key) deploy can be verified via /version commit"]	Fix deploy observability: bake RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA into Docker image so /version reports actual commit	## Problem\nProduction /health and /version return commit:unknown and build_time:unknown. The code in src/lib/build-info.ts correctly reads RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA but the Railway Dockerfile build never passes this to the container. This means:\n- No one can verify what code is running in production\n- All 62 improvement proposals with deploy-verification acceptance criteria are structurally untestable\n- Incident response cannot confirm deploy state\n\n## Root Cause\n1. Railway Dockerfile builder injects RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA as a build-time variable, but the Dockerfile does not consume it (no ARG, no ENV)\n2. The Dockerfile compiles with RUN npx tsc but CMD runs tsx src/index.ts instead of node dist/index.js, so import.meta.url reports source mode\n\n## Fix\n1. Add to Dockerfile after FROM:\n   ARG RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA\n   ARG RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_CREATED_AT\n   ENV PARSE_COMMIT_SHA=$RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA\n   ENV PARSE_BUILD_TIME=$RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_CREATED_AT\n2. If tsx runtime is required for Prisma ESM, keep tsx but still bake commit SHA via ENV — runtime:source is acceptable if documented\n3. If possible, switch CMD to node dist/index.js for compiled runtime (runtime:dist) — test Prisma ESM compatibility first\n4. Redeploy and verify /version returns actual commit SHA\n\n## Safety Gates\n- ARG/ENV changes are non-mutating to application logic\n- No billing, security policy, API key, or customer data changes\n- Verify Prisma ESM imports work if switching from tsx to node\n- Test locally with docker build --build-arg RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA=test123\n- Rollback: revert Dockerfile ARG/ENV lines — no data migration needed\n\n## Verification\n- Production GET /version returns non-unknown commit SHA\n- Production GET /version returns non-unknown build_time\n- Re-run parse_business_state_context.py — ops_reliability checks pass with real commit metadata	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Master Key:master	Master Key:master	2026-08-09 01:31:42.183	2026-08-09 01:31:42.183
cmsl7arvc0000g61ewct605ok	elon-migration-gap-13-tables-unmigrated-2026-08-09	P0 DEPLOY BLOCKER: 13 tables + 5 columns have zero migration files — production deploy of current code will break the entire admin plane	reliability	10	proposed	high-if-ignored	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"source": "live collector run 2026-08-09T00:05Z plus local DB introspection", "local_head": "d8d5351", "live_symptom": "admin.dashboard.snapshot returns 500 (screeningEvent.findMany fails), admin.billing.anomaly.scan returns 500 (subscription.findMany fails with include:{apiKey} hitting api_keys.role column drift)", "commits_ahead": 23, "production_commit": "a26b3761a4530912a3f3a064d814e7c70a5bb128", "total_table_count": 27, "unmigrated_tables": ["agent_data_grants", "agent_registry", "compliance_exports", "compliance_receipts", "data_sources", "delegation_chains", "egress_rules", "organizations", "policy_revisions", "siem_configs", "signed_identities", "sso_providers", "volume_budgets"], "unmigrated_columns": {"api_keys": ["role", "org_id"], "screening_events": ["environment", "would_block", "enforcement_mode"]}, "migrated_table_count": 15}	Production is frozen 23 commits behind local. Every commit after a26b376 added schema without a migration SQL file. Deploying current code will crash every admin action that touches any of these 13 tables or the 5 unmigrated columns. This blocks ALL SaaS-readiness work: no deploy, no fix, no ship.	["prisma/migrations/011_add_compliance_and_org_tables.sql exists with CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS for all 13 unmigrated tables matching the Prisma schema exactly", "prisma/migrations/012_add_missing_columns.sql adds api_keys.role, api_keys.org_id, screening_events.environment, screening_events.would_block, screening_events.enforcement_mode", "All migration files are idempotent (IF NOT EXISTS / ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS)", "Running all migrations sequentially on a fresh empty DB produces the same schema as the local development DB", "admin.dashboard.snapshot returns 200 on production after deploy", "admin.billing.anomaly.scan returns 200 on production after deploy"]	Create migration files for 13 unmigrated tables + 5 unmigrated columns	## Problem\n13 tables and 5 columns exist in schema.prisma but have NO migration SQL files.\nProduction DB (at commit a26b376) lacks them entirely. Local DB has them (created via prisma db push or ad hoc SQL).\nThe gap was introduced by feature commits between 58c7698 and d8d5351.\n\n## Unmigrated Tables (13)\nagent_data_grants, agent_registry, compliance_exports, compliance_receipts,\ndata_sources, delegation_chains, egress_rules, organizations, policy_revisions,\nsiem_configs, signed_identities, sso_providers, volume_budgets\n\n## Unmigrated Columns (5)\n- api_keys: role (varchar, default developer), org_id (varchar, nullable)\n- screening_events: environment (varchar, default production), would_block (boolean nullable), enforcement_mode (varchar nullable)\n\n## Fix\n1. Generate prisma/migrations/011_add_compliance_and_org_tables.sql with CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS for all 13 tables.\n2. Generate prisma/migrations/012_add_missing_columns.sql with ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS for all 5 columns.\n3. Both files must be idempotent (safe to run on local DB which already has them).\n4. Verify: npx prisma generate succeeds, then run a count() query on each of the 13 models.\n\n## Safety gates\n- Do NOT run prisma migrate reset on any DB\n- Do NOT run prisma db push (it creates schema without migration history)\n- Test migrations on a fresh throwaway DB first\n- Production deployment of migrations requires Dannys explicit approval\n- After deploy: verify admin.dashboard.snapshot and admin.billing.anomaly.scan return 200	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-08-09 02:46:05.208	2026-08-09 02:46:05.208
cmsl9ki6u0000mt1efgnbgelr	deploy-commit-traceability-2026-08-09	Inject git commit SHA and build timestamp into /version at deploy time — eliminate commit:unknown in production	ops-reliability	8	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"probe": "GET https://www.parsethis.ai/version", "impact": "No deploy can be verified against a specific commit. Every post-deploy smoke protocol in the ops runbook references /version commit verification and silently fails. Incident response cannot correlate bugs to deploys. Investor diligence deploy-hygiene check fails.", "observed": {"commit": "unknown", "runtime": "source", "build_time": "unknown"}, "root_cause": "build-info.ts reads RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA, PARSE_COMMIT_SHA, GIT_COMMIT etc from process.env, but none are injected during Railway build/run"}	Unblocks all deploy verification workflows, enables incident-to-commit correlation, satisfies investor diligence deploy hygiene gate, and makes every future admin-operations smoke test meaningful instead of theater.	["GET /version on production returns a non-unknown commit hash (7+ hex chars) matching the deployed git HEAD", "GET /version returns a build_time timestamp (ISO 8601) within minutes of the Railway deploy", "After a code change + redeploy, /version commit changes to match the new HEAD", "Local dev still works without commit injection (falls back gracefully to unknown)", "No secrets, API keys, or internal paths are exposed in the /version payload", "npm run build and npm run typecheck pass with the change"]	Wire Railway build-time git commit injection into /version endpoint	## Objective\nEliminate `commit: unknown` in production /version by injecting the git commit SHA and build timestamp at deploy time.\n\n## Context\n`src/lib/build-info.ts` already reads `RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA`, `PARSE_COMMIT_SHA`, `GIT_COMMIT`, `SOURCE_VERSION`, and `PARSE_BUILD_TIME` / `RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_CREATED_AT`. None are set in the Railway environment. The fix is to ensure at least one commit source and one build-time source are populated.\n\n## Implementation options (pick the simplest that works)\n1. **Prebuild script (recommended):** Add a `prebuild` npm script that runs `git rev-parse HEAD > .commit-sha` and `date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ > .build-time`, then have `build-info.ts` read those files as a fallback before env vars. This works on Railway source-sync deploys where RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA may not be available.\n2. **Railway env var:** Set `PARSE_COMMIT_SHA=$RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA` as a Railway variable. Simpler config but only works if Railway provides the SHA var during build.\n\n## Safety gates\n- Do NOT expose full git remote, branch names, or author emails in /version (security)\n- Do NOT break local dev — fallback to `unknown` when no commit source exists\n- Do NOT add the change to any auth-gated path; /version must remain public\n- Verify with `npm run typecheck && npm run build` before deploy\n- Post-deploy: verify `curl https://www.parsethis.ai/version` returns non-unknown commit\n\n## Files to touch\n- `src/lib/build-info.ts` (add file-read fallback)\n- `package.json` (add prebuild script, if option 1)\n- Potentially `.gitignore` (ignore generated `.commit-sha` / `.build-time` files)\n\n## Verification\n- `npm run build` produces dist/ with the change\n- Local: `npm run start` → /version shows local git HEAD\n- Deploy → `curl https://www.parsethis.ai/version` → commit matches deployed HEAD	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Master Key:master	Master Key:master	2026-08-09 03:49:38.454	2026-08-09 03:49:38.454
cmslbyvov00158y1ekb336kv5	elon-tsx-dev-runtime-in-production-root-cause-2026-08-09	P1 ROOT CAUSE: Production runs tsx (dev JIT compiler) not compiled dist/ — Dockerfile builds then throws away output, wastes CPU, adds latency, breaks commit injection	reliability	8	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"source": "live /version probe + Dockerfile + railway.toml inspection 2026-08-09T04:50Z", "dockerfile_cmd": "CMD [\\"./node_modules/.bin/tsx\\", \\"src/index.ts\\"]", "version_endpoint": "https://www.parsethis.ai/version returns runtime:source, commit:unknown, build_time:unknown", "dockerfile_comment": "# Keep tsx for runtime (Prisma 7.x ESM requires TypeScript imports)", "package_json_build": "prisma generate && tsc && node scripts/patch-prisma-dist-imports.mjs — this full build is what should run", "dockerfile_runs_tsc": "RUN npx tsc — compiles to dist/ but output is never used", "downstream_symptoms": ["commit:unknown on every deploy", "runtime:source in health/version", "slower cold starts from JIT TypeScript transpilation", "patch-prisma-dist-imports.mjs never applied"], "railway_startcommand": "npx tsx src/index.ts", "dockerfile_missing_patch": "patch-prisma-dist-imports.mjs (which fixes Prisma ESM for dist/) is never run in Docker build", "existing_proposals_this_unblocks": ["elon-deploy-observability-zero-commit-metadata-2026-08-09", "deploy-commit-traceability-2026-08-09"]}	Production serves customer traffic through tsx, a development JIT TypeScript compiler. The Dockerfile runs tsc (wasted CPU), then ignores the compiled dist/ and runs tsx instead because of a Prisma 7 ESM workaround. This is the root cause of commit:unknown, runtime:source, and slower startup. The fix is to run the full npm build (which includes patch-prisma-dist-imports.mjs) in the Dockerfile and switch CMD to node dist/index.js. This unblocks 2 existing proposals about deploy observability and makes every cold start faster.	["Dockerfile RUN step includes full build: prisma generate && tsc && node scripts/patch-prisma-dist-imports.mjs", "Dockerfile CMD changed from tsx src/index.ts to node dist/index.js", "railway.toml startCommand updated to node dist/index.js (or removed so Dockerfile CMD is used)", "After deploy: /version returns runtime:dist not runtime:source", "After deploy: all core endpoints (/health, /v1/keys/generate, /v1/parse with valid key, /v1/screen-output) return expected status codes", "Cold start time measured before and after — document improvement", "If patch-prisma-dist-imports.mjs fails, diagnose and fix the Prisma ESM import path rather than reverting to tsx"]	Switch production from tsx dev runtime to compiled dist/ with Prisma ESM patch	## Problem\nProduction runs `tsx src/index.ts` (a dev JIT compiler) instead of `node dist/index.js`. The Dockerfile runs `npx tsc` but never uses the output. The workaround comment says Prisma 7.x ESM requires TypeScript imports, but `scripts/patch-prisma-dist-imports.mjs` already exists to fix this for compiled output — it is just never run in the Docker build.\n\n## Fix\n1. Change Dockerfile build step from `RUN npx tsc` to `RUN npm run build` (which runs prisma generate + tsc + patch-prisma-dist-imports.mjs)\n2. Change Dockerfile CMD from `["./node_modules/.bin/tsx", "src/index.ts"]` to `["node", "dist/index.js"]`\n3. Update railway.toml startCommand from `npx tsx src/index.ts` to `node dist/index.js` (or remove it so Dockerfile CMD is authoritative)\n4. Deploy and verify /version shows runtime:dist\n5. Run full smoke: /health, /v1/keys/generate, /v1/parse, /v1/screen-output, /admin\n\n## Safety gates\n- Do NOT deploy without verifying all core endpoints work post-build\n- If patch-prisma-dist-imports.mjs fails, debug the ESM import issue in dist/ rather than reverting to tsx\n- Test the full build locally first: npm run build && node dist/index.js\n- Production deploy requires Danny approval\n- Keep tsx available for dev (npm run dev stays unchanged)	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Master Key:master	Master Key:master	2026-08-09 04:56:48.367	2026-08-09 04:56:48.367
cmslsf164004y9i1ern2zeule	elon-landing-hero-installs-nonexistent-npm-package-2026-08-09	P0 CONVERSION: Landing hero + docs say `npm install @parsethis/sdk` — package does not exist on npm	conversion_friction	10	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"npm_404": "npm registry returns 404 for @parsethis/sdk — package never published", "blog_post": "how-to-secure-claude-code-agents.md references `npm install @parsethis/sdk` + `import { ParseClient } from '@parsethis/sdk'` (lines 97, 107, 265)", "quickstart": "https://www.parsethis.ai/docs/quickstart says `npm install @parsethis/sdk` + `import { wrap } from '@parsethis/sdk'` (content/docs/quickstart.md lines 71, 163, 167)", "smoke_test": "Verified @parsethis/prompt-guard 0.1.1 installed from npm: ESM import works, exports {PromptGuard, PromptGuardClient}, no wrap()/ParseClient. CommonJS require fails (ESM-only exports map).", "landing_page": "https://www.parsethis.ai/ renders 'npm install @parsethis/sdk' as hero CTA (src/pages/landing.ts lines 82, 476, 513)", "local_code_exists": "packages/parse-sdk/ has TS+Python source with wrap()/ParseClient but was never published and has no package.json", "actual_published_package": "@parsethis/prompt-guard v0.1.1 — exports PromptGuardClient (screenPrompt, screenOutput, getPolicy), NOT wrap() or ParseClient"}	Every visitor or agent following the primary install path gets `npm ERR! 404 Not Found`. The hero CTA on the landing page is a broken npm command. This is the highest-friction first-mile failure because it blocks the literal first action after reading the landing page. Agents parsing /llms.txt or /get-started and trying the SDK path also fail.	["All references to @parsethis/sdk replaced with the real package name OR @parsethis/sdk published to npm with working wrap()/ParseClient exports", "Landing page hero (landing.ts), quickstart docs, and blog post all reference a package that returns 200 from npm registry", "Import examples use real exports: `import { PromptGuardClient } from '@parsethis/prompt-guard'` or publish @parsethis/sdk with the documented API", "ESM + CJS dual support in published package (prompt-guard currently ESM-only, fails on require())", "Copy-button or install snippet on landing page verified against npm registry before deploy", "Brand-lint passes with corrected package names"]	Fix nonexistent npm package in landing hero + docs + blog — @parsethis/sdk doesn't exist	## Problem\nThe landing page hero CTA, quickstart docs, and blog post all instruct users to `npm install @parsethis/sdk` and import `{ wrap }` or `{ ParseClient }` from it. This package does not exist on npm (404). The actual published package is `@parsethis/prompt-guard` v0.1.1, which exports `{ PromptGuard, PromptGuardClient }` — no `wrap()`, no `ParseClient`.\n\n## Files to fix\n- `src/pages/landing.ts` (lines 82, 476, 513) — hero CTA and install snippets\n- `content/docs/quickstart.md` (lines 71, 163, 167) — SDK install + import examples\n- `content/blog/agent-security/how-to-secure-claude-code-agents.md` (lines 97, 99, 107, 265) — npm/pnpm install + import\n\n## Two acceptable approaches\n**Option A (fastest):** Change all references to `@parsethis/prompt-guard` and use real imports: `import { PromptGuardClient } from '@parsethis/prompt-guard'`. Update code examples to use `client.screenPrompt()` / `client.screenOutput()` instead of `wrap()`.\n\n**Option B (publish):** Build and publish `packages/parse-sdk/` as `@parsethis/sdk` with `wrap()` and `ParseClient` exports. This requires adding package.json, build pipeline, and npm publish access.\n\nRecommend Option A first (unblocks everyone in one deploy), Option B as follow-up if the `wrap()` ergonomic API is preferred.\n\n## Safety gates\n- DO NOT change API endpoints, auth, billing, or security policy\n- DO NOT modify the OpenAPI spec or llms.txt in this task (separate proposals exist for those)\n- Verify every npm package name referenced in the deploy returns 200 from registry.npmjs.org before shipping\n- Run `npm run brand-lint` and `npm run claims-lint` after changes\n- Run `npm run typecheck && npm run build` before deploy\n- This is docs/landing copy only — no DB migrations, no env changes, no provider mutations	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Master Key:master	Master Key:master	2026-08-09 12:37:15.82	2026-08-09 12:37:15.82
cmsle724l000ef81ere7arww1	deploy-traceability-version-commit-sha-2026-08-09	Wire RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA so /version shows real deploy commit	ops-reliability	8	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"impact": "Cannot verify deployed commit during post-deploy smoke, incident response, or investor diligence", "code_path": "src/lib/build-info.ts reads RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA but env var is not populated in production", "health_check": "https://www.parsethis.ai/health returns status ok", "git_head_local": "ce19ad0", "version_endpoint": "GET /version returns deployment.commit=unknown, build_time=unknown"}	critical-for-observability	["GET /version returns a real commit SHA (not unknown) after next Railway deploy", "GET /version returns build_time timestamp", "Admin canary checks can verify deploy matches intended commit"]	Wire deploy commit SHA into /version metadata	## Problem\nProduction /version always returns commit=unknown, build_time=unknown. The code in src/lib/build-info.ts reads RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA but Railway is not populating it.\n\n## Likely Root Cause\nEither the Railway project is not GitHub-connected (deploys via railway up without git context) or the env var name changed.\n\n## Fix Options (pick the simplest that works)\n1. Ensure Railway project is connected to GitHub repo so RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA is auto-populated\n2. If not GitHub-connected, set PARSE_COMMIT_SHA in a railway build hook or Nixpacks build step: echo $RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA > /app/.commit\n3. Also capture RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_CREATED_AT for build_time\n\n## Safety Gates\n- Read-only change to deployment metadata surfacing\n- No billing/security/policy mutations\n- No customer-facing API contract changes\n- Verify with: curl https://www.parsethis.ai/version after deploy\n\n## Acceptance Test\ncurl -s https://www.parsethis.ai/version | jq .deployment.commit — must NOT equal unknown	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Master Key:master	Master Key:master	2026-08-09 05:59:09.189	2026-08-09 05:59:09.189
