em\n67 improvement proposals sit in 'proposed' status. Zero have been actioned. The review flow requires individual confirm() dialogs per card — at 67 items this is a UX tax that makes the inbox unprocessable in a single session.\n\n## Solution\nAdd batch triage to the proposal section in src/pages/admin.ts:\n1. Add a 'Select all' checkbox at the top of the proposal list.\n2. Add a checkbox to each proposal-card.\n3. Add batch action buttons: 'Approve selected', 'Reject selected', 'Defer selected'.\n4. Batch actions show ONE prompt() for a reason/decision, then call admin.improvement_proposal.update_status for each selected id.\n5. Refresh the proposal list after batch action completes.\n6. Keep existing per-card buttons for single-item review.\n\n## Implementation location\nsrc/pages/admin.ts — the renderProposals() function and the click handler for proposal actions.\n\n## Safety gates\n- No backend changes required — uses existing admin.improvement_proposal.update_status action\n- No deploy, no billing change, no security policy change\n- Pure frontend enhancement to existing /admin page\n- Test batch behavior with npm run typecheck and a focused test if feasible\n- Requires Danny's deploy approval (the repo is 23+ commits ahead of production)\n\n## Why this matters now\nEvery other proposal in the inbox is blocked on human review. Without batch triage, the review bottleneck persists and no improvement proposal — however excellent — converts to action. This is the lever that unblocks 67 items at once.	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Master Key:master	Master Key:master	2026-08-09 07:02:40.746	2026-08-09 07:02:40.746
cmslirwgg0000pn1evfrpjr8j	elon-x402-disabled-401-instead-of-402-revenue-dead-2026-08-09	P0 REVENUE: x402 globally disabled (enabled:false, no facilitator/wallet) — every billable endpoint returns 401 instead of 402, zero agents can pay-per-call	revenue	10	proposed	medium	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"probes": {"GET /v1/pricing": {"payTo": "not_configured", "enabled": false, "facilitator": "not_configured", "endpoints_listed": 5}, "expected_behavior": "HTTP 402 with accepts[] payment requirements per x402 spec", "llms.txt x402_mentions": 7, "POST /v1/chat (no auth)": {"code": "auth.required", "http_status": 401}, "POST /v1/parse (no auth)": {"code": "auth.required", "http_status": 401}, "POST /v1/analyze (no auth)": {"code": "auth.required", "http_status": 401}, "pricing_manifest_endpoints": 5, "openapi_402_response_documented": true, "POST /v1/screen-output (no auth)": {"code": "auth.required", "http_status": 401}}, "timestamp": "2026-08-09T08:06:00Z", "root_cause": "x402 facilitator integration not configured in production — payTo wallet unset, facilitator endpoint unset, enabled flag false. Billable endpoints fall through to bearer auth check and return 401 before the x402 payment intercept fires."}	Autonomous agents discovering Parse via llms.txt, MCP manifest, or OpenAPI are told to use x402 for no-signup instant access. When they call /v1/parse without a bearer key, they get HTTP 401 'Authentication required' instead of HTTP 402 'Payment Required' with payment requirements. The agent cannot proceed — it can't pay, and it doesn't know a free key path exists. This kills the primary zero-friction conversion path for autonomous agent customers. Every agent integration guide, pricing manifest, and llms.txt advertises x402 as a working payment method, but production returns enabled:false with payTo:not_configured and facilitator:not_configured. This is a false-advertising + dead-revenue-path bug.	["GET /v1/pricing returns enabled:true with a real payTo wallet and facilitator URL", "POST /v1/parse without bearer key or payment-signature returns HTTP 402 with accepts[] payment requirements (not 401)", "POST /v1/screen-output without auth returns HTTP 402 with accepts[] (not 401)", "POST /v1/analyze without auth returns HTTP 402 with accepts[] (not 401)", "POST /v1/chat without auth returns HTTP 402 with accepts[] (not 401)", "An agent paying the advertised USDC amount receives a 200/202 screening result", "llms.txt and OpenAPI 402 response descriptions match actual production behavior"]	Configure x402 facilitator + wallet in production so billable endpoints return 402 not 401	## Problem\nx402 is advertised as Parse's zero-friction payment path for autonomous agents but is globally disabled in production. Every billable endpoint returns HTTP 401 instead of HTTP 402.\n\n## Root Cause (live evidence)\n- GET /v1/pricing → enabled:false, payTo:not_configured, facilitator:not_configured\n- POST /v1/parse (no auth) → 401 auth.required (should be 402 with accepts[])\n- POST /v1/screen-output (no auth) → 401 auth.required (should be 402)\n- POST /v1/analyze (no auth) → 401 auth.required (should be 402)\n- POST /v1/chat (no auth) → 401 auth.required (should be 402)\n\n## Fix\n1. Set the x402 facilitator URL and payTo wallet in production Railway env vars\n2. Set enabled:true in the pricing/payment config\n3. Ensure the x402 payment interceptor runs BEFORE the bearer auth check on billable endpoints\n4. Verify each billable endpoint returns 402 with valid accepts[] when no auth/payment is present\n5. Test end-to-end: agent calls /v1/parse without auth, gets 402, signs USDC payment, retries, gets 200\n\n## Safety Gates\n- Do NOT change bearer key auth behavior — existing API key holders must continue working unchanged\n- Do NOT change pricing amounts — match existing /v1/pricing manifest\n- Test with a small USDC amount first if real facilitator settlement is enabled\n- Verify x402 does not bypass screening policy or rate limits\n- Gated action: setting up a real crypto wallet/facilitator requires Danny's approval for the wallet address and facilitator service	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	2026-08-09 08:07:20.08	2026-08-09 08:07:20.08
cmsll858n00259i1efxajc0j2	elon-onboarding-dashboard-cookie-bridge-2026-08-09	P0 ONBOARDING DEAD-END: /get-started sends users to /dashboard/agents which returns 401 — keygen key is in localStorage but admin cookie is never set	conversion_friction	9	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"root_cause": "/get-started JS stores the generated key in localStorage but never calls POST /admin/login to set the parse_admin_key cookie. The Go to Dashboard button is a plain href that navigates directly to /dashboard/agents, which SSR-renders with authMiddleware and finds no cookie.", "probe_results": {"auth_middleware": "src/auth.ts:155 — checks Authorization header then parse_admin_key cookie; localStorage pfa_key is never read server-side", "keygen_endpoint": "POST /v1/keys/generate returns 201 with full key", "get_started_page": "200 OK — keygen works, generates key, stores in localStorage", "dashboard_response": "401 Authentication required — no cookie, no Authorization header", "admin_login_endpoint": "POST /admin/login with {api_key} sets parse_admin_key cookie and returns {ok:true, redirect:/dashboard/agents}", "go_to_dashboard_link": "href=/dashboard/agents — plain anchor, no cookie bridge"}, "reproduced_at": "2026-08-09T09:12Z", "affected_users": "100% of self-service users who complete keygen on /get-started"}	Every new user who completes the activation flow (generates key, tests API, clicks Go to Dashboard) hits a 401 wall. The key sits in localStorage but /dashboard/agents SSR requires the parse_admin_key cookie. Zero users can reach the dashboard from onboarding without manually visiting /admin/login. This breaks the entire self-serve activation funnel at the final step.	["After generating a key on /get-started, clicking Go to Dashboard lands on /dashboard/agents with 200, not 401", "If a user already has a key in localStorage and revisits /get-started, the dashboard link still works (auto-bridges cookie on load)", "If the key is expired or revoked, the bridge fails gracefully and redirects to /admin/login with a helpful message", "No secrets exposed: the key is sent to POST /admin/login via HTTPS only, never in URL params", "The fix does not change auth middleware, cookie attributes, or security boundaries"]	Bridge /get-started keygen to /admin/login cookie so dashboard access works	## Problem\n/get-started generates an API key, stores it in localStorage as `pfa_key`, and links to /dashboard/agents — but the dashboard requires the `parse_admin_key` cookie, which is never set. Result: 401 for 100% of new users.\n\n## Fix (estimated 15 lines of JS in src/pages/get-started.ts)\n1. Add a JS function `bridgeAdminCookie(key)` that POSTs `{api_key: key}` to `/admin/login`.\n2. On page load, if `localStorage.getItem(pfa_key)` exists, call `bridgeAdminCookie()` proactively so the cookie is set before the user clicks.\n3. On the `gs-go-dashboard` click handler, call `bridgeAdminCookie()` if not already bridged, then navigate.\n4. If the bridge fails (expired/revoked key), redirect to `/admin/login` with a note.\n\n## Safety gates\n- Do NOT change auth middleware, cookie attributes, or security boundaries.\n- Do NOT add the key to URL params — only POST body over HTTPS.\n- The cookie is already HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Lax, 30-day expiry — no changes needed.\n- Test: generate fresh key on /get-started → click dashboard → expect 200.\n- Test: expired key in localStorage → click dashboard → expect graceful redirect to /admin/login.\n- Regression: existing /admin/login form-based login must still work independently.\n\n## Files\n- `src/pages/get-started.ts` (or wherever the get-started SSR page JS is rendered)\n- Existing `POST /admin/login` endpoint is the only server-side dependency — no API changes needed.	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Master Key:master	Master Key:master	2026-08-09 09:15:57.191	2026-08-09 09:15:57.191
