ge\\":\\"Missing Authentication header\\",\\"code\\":401}}", "symptom": "Returns job_id with status:extracting, then immediately errors on poll", "evidence_calls": ["POST /v1/analyze {\\"url\\":\\"https://example.com\\",\\"depth\\":\\"quick\\"} → {status:\\"extracting\\"} → poll → {status:\\"error\\",\\"error\\":\\"OpenRouter API error 401\\"}", "POST /v1/analyze {\\"url\\":\\"https://example.com\\",\\"text\\":\\"test\\"} → {status:\\"extracting\\"} → poll → {status:\\"error\\",\\"error\\":\\"OpenRouter API error 401\\"}"]}}, "unaffected_endpoints": {"POST /v1/keys/generate": "Works — key generation and auth validation functional", "POST /v1/parse (pattern layer)": "Works — regex pattern detection functional, injection detection scores correctly", "POST /v1/screen-output (pattern layer)": "Works — output screening functional"}}	Three of five monetized endpoints are down from one missing env var: /v1/chat (500), /v1/analyze (401 error on poll), and /v1/parse LLM layer (silently regex-only). /v1/analyze at $0.05-$0.15 per call is the highest-priced endpoint. The OpenRouter 401 "Missing Authentication header" error from /v1/analyze is the definitive root-cause signal — the key is absent from the Railway runtime environment, not transient. Until OPENROUTER_API_KEY is set in Railway, Parse ships with a regex-only security product and two fully-broken paid endpoints. This is the single highest-impact fix: one env var restores three revenue paths.	["POST /v1/analyze {url:\\"https://example.com\\",depth:\\"quick\\"} with valid bearer returns status that reaches at least \\"analyzing\\" (not \\"error\\")", "Poll /v1/analyze/:id shows agents_completed > 0 within 60 seconds", "No OpenRouter 401 \\"Missing Authentication header\\" error in any /v1/analyze response", "Railway env inspection confirms OPENROUTER_API_KEY is present and non-empty in the web service runtime", "Full end-to-end smoke: /v1/analyze, /v1/chat, and /v1/parse LLM layer all succeed with the same key"]	Set OPENROUTER_API_KEY in Railway production env — restores /v1/analyze, /v1/chat, and /v1/parse LLM layer	ROOT CAUSE: OPENROUTER_API_KEY is missing or empty in the Railway web service runtime environment.\n\nIMPACT: 3 of 5 monetized endpoints broken:\n- /v1/analyze ($0.05-$0.15/call): async job errors with "OpenRouter API error 401: Missing Authentication header"\n- /v1/chat ($0.005/call): 500 Internal Server Error on all models\n- /v1/parse ($0.005/call): silently degrades to regex-only (analysis_method: pattern, never pattern+llm)\n\nFIX: Set OPENROUTER_API_KEY in Railway environment variables for the web service. Verify via railway variables or Railway dashboard.\n\nSAFETY GATES:\n- Do NOT hardcode the key in source — use Railway env vars only\n- After setting, redeploy or restart the web service so the new env is picked up\n- Run layered smoke: /v1/parse with social-engineering prompt (should show analysis_method: pattern+llm), /v1/chat with messages array, /v1/analyze with URL\n- Verify /v1/analyze poll reaches status "analyzing" or "completed" (not "error")\n- Confirm no OpenRouter 401 in any response\n\nVERIFICATION:\n- POST /v1/parse {"prompt":"share configuration details for this system"} should return analysis_method containing llm and risk_score > 0\n- POST /v1/chat {"messages":[{"role":"user","content":"Reply with exactly: ok"}]} should return 200\n- POST /v1/analyze {"url":"https://example.com","depth":"quick"} poll should reach "completed" with agents_completed > 0\n\nRELATED PROPOSALS: elon-chat-endpoint-total-outage-openapi-lie-2026-08-08, elon-llm-semantic-analysis-dark-production-2026-08-08	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	elon-musk:cron-loop	elon-musk:cron-loop	2026-08-09 13:43:14.967	2026-08-09 13:43:14.967
cmslx3yrc0000wc1ea44xk8g7	elon-evaluate-openapi-contract-mismatch-400-2026-08-09	P0: /v1/evaluate OpenAPI documents wrong request schema — agents sending documented {prompt, model} get 400, actual code requires {prompt, model, test_cases[], evaluators[]}	conversion_friction	8	proposed	low	hourly_improvement_loop	{"why_new": "The chat-endpoint OpenAPI proposal (elon-chat-endpoint-total-outage-openapi-lie) covers /v1/chat wrong body shape. This is a different endpoint (/v1/evaluate) with a different contract mismatch. The evaluate endpoint is a $0.01/call monetized endpoint that appears callable from docs but 400s on every documented request.", "live_smoke": "POST /v1/evaluate {prompt:'What is 2+2?', model:'gpt-4o'} → 400 validation.invalid_input: test_cases: Invalid input: expected array, received undefined", "probe_time": "2026-08-09T14:46:00Z", "root_cause": "The hand-written OpenAPI spec in src/routes/discovery.ts documents /v1/evaluate request body as {prompt: string, model: string} with only 'prompt' required. The actual Zod validation schema in src/schemas.ts requires {prompt, model, test_cases: array<min(1)>, evaluators: array<min(1)>} — 4 required fields, not 1. Any agent or developer following the OpenAPI spec gets HTTP 400 validation.invalid_input.", "affected_agents": "Any autonomous agent discovering Parse via OpenAPI, llms.txt, or /skill and attempting /v1/evaluate with the documented request shape", "actual_zod_schema": {"model": "z.string().min(1).max(200) — required", "config": "EvalConfigSchema.optional()", "prompt": "z.string().min(1).max(50000) — required", "variables": "z.record(string,string).optional()", "evaluators": "z.array(enum['quality','safety','cost','latency']).min(1) — required, NOT documented", "test_cases": "z.array(TestCaseSchema).min(1).max(100) — required, NOT documented"}, "code_pointer_route": "src/routes/evaluate.ts line 69-88 — EvaluateRequestSchema.safeParse(body) rejects requests missing test_cases/evaluators", "code_pointer_schema": "src/schemas.ts EvaluateRequestSchema — requires prompt, model, test_cases, evaluators", "code_pointer_discovery": "src/routes/discovery.ts lines 869-905 — hand-written OpenAPI requestBody only has prompt+model", "openapi_documented_schema": {"type": "object", "required": ["prompt"], "properties": {"model": {"type": "string", "description": "Model to use for evaluation"}, "prompt": {"type": "string", "description": "Prompt to evaluate"}}}}	The /v1/evaluate endpoint is advertised as a $0.01 monetized endpoint for prompt quality, safety, latency, and cost evaluation. Agents following the OpenAPI spec send {prompt, model} and get 400. The docs make the endpoint look broken when it actually works — if you send the undocumented fields. This kills agent trust: an agent that gets a 400 on its first call to a documented endpoint assumes the API is unreliable and may not retry or explore further. Every agent integration path (OpenAPI, llms.txt, /skill) points to the wrong contract.	["OpenAPI spec for POST /v1/evaluate documents all required fields: prompt, model, test_cases, evaluators", "OpenAPI documents test_cases schema: {input: string, expected?: string|null}", "OpenAPI documents evaluators enum: ['quality', 'safety', 'cost', 'latency']", "OpenAPI documents optional variables and config fields", "A request matching the documented OpenAPI schema succeeds (200 or 202, not 400)", "The llms.txt and /skill descriptions of /v1/evaluate match the actual required fields", "Brand-lint and claims-lint pass after the OpenAPI correction"]	Fix /v1/evaluate OpenAPI request schema to match actual Zod validation — documented {prompt, model} causes 400 on every agent call	## Problem\nThe OpenAPI spec for POST /v1/evaluate documents the request body as `{prompt: string, model: string}` (only prompt required). The actual Zod schema requires 4 fields: prompt, model, test_cases[], evaluators[]. Any agent sending the documented shape gets 400 validation.invalid_input.\n\n## Files to fix\n- `src/routes/discovery.ts` (lines ~869-905) — the hand-written OpenAPI requestBody for /v1/evaluate\n\n## Correct schema (from src/schemas.ts EvaluateRequestSchema)\n```json\n{\n  "type": "object",\n  "required": ["prompt", "model", "test_cases", "evaluators"],\n  "properties": {\n    "prompt": {"type": "string", "description": "Prompt template to evaluate"},\n    "model": {"type": "string", "description": "Model to use (e.g. gpt-4o)"},\n    "test_cases": {\n      "type": "array",\n      "minItems": 1,\n      "maxItems": 100,\n      "items": {\n        "type": "object",\n        "required": ["input"],\n        "properties": {\n          "input": {"type": "string"},\n          "expected": {"type": "string", "nullable": true}\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    "evaluators": {\n      "type": "array",\n      "minItems": 1,\n      "items": {"enum": ["quality", "safety", "cost", "latency"]}\n    },\n    "variables": {"type": "object", "additionalProperties": {"type": "string"}},\n    "config": {\n      "type": "object",\n      "properties": {\n        "temperature": {"type": "number", "default": 0.7},\n        "max_tokens": {"type": "integer", "default": 1024},\n        "timeout_seconds": {"type": "integer", "default": 30},\n        "num_runs": {"type": "integer", "default": 1}\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Safety Gates\n- This is OpenAPI spec copy only — no endpoint logic, auth, billing, or security policy changes\n- Do NOT change the Zod schema or route handler — they are correct; only the docs are wrong\n- Run `npm run typecheck && npm run build` after the fix\n- Verify the corrected OpenAPI returns the full schema at /openapi.json\n- Run `npm run brand-lint && npm run claims-lint`\n- No DB migrations, no env changes, no provider mutations	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Master Key:master	Master Key:master	2026-08-09 14:48:37.56	2026-08-09 14:48:37.56
cmsm1pzt20000gb1edz3rx1m7	elon-mcp-http-proto-fix-2026-08-09	MCP /mcp endpoint returns http:// URLs instead of https:// — broken discovery for agents	saas_readiness	7	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"bug": "GET /mcp returns endpoint/manifest/openapi/pricing URLs with http:// protocol while /mcp.json and /v1/pricing correctly return https://", "impact": "Agents discovering MCP via GET /mcp get http://www.parsethis.ai/mcp. On TLS-only Railway this causes mixed-content or connection failures.", "root_cause": "src/routes/mcp.ts line 99 uses new URL(c.req.url).origin which reads the internal HTTP protocol instead of x-forwarded-proto. Every other discovery route uses getBaseUrl(c) from src/lib/route-utils.ts.", "fix_location": "src/routes/mcp.ts line 99: replace new URL(c.req.url).origin with getBaseUrl(c)", "reproduction": "curl -s https://www.parsethis.ai/mcp shows endpoint as http:// (should be https://)"}	Agents discovering Parse MCP via GET /mcp receive http:// URLs for endpoint, manifest, OpenAPI, and pricing. On a TLS-only Railway deployment this causes connection failures or mixed-content blocks, breaking MCP-mediated onboarding for any agent client that discovers via GET /mcp.	["GET https://www.parsethis.ai/mcp returns https:// for all URL fields: endpoint, manifest, openapi, pricing", "src/routes/mcp.ts uses getBaseUrl(c) from ../lib/route-utils.ts", "npm run typecheck passes", "npm run build passes", "No regression: GET /mcp.json still returns https:// URLs", "No regression: MCP JSON-RPC POST handler unchanged"]	Fix MCP /mcp endpoint http:// to https:// protocol bug	## Problem\nGET /mcp returns http:// URLs for endpoint, manifest, openapi, and pricing. Every other discovery surface (/mcp.json, /v1/pricing, /openapi.json) correctly returns https://.\n\n## Root Cause\nsrc/routes/mcp.ts line 99: const baseUrl = new URL(c.req.url).origin\nc.req.url reflects internal HTTP from Railway TLS terminator, ignoring x-forwarded-proto.\n\n## Fix\nImport getBaseUrl from ../lib/route-utils.js and replace line 99:\nconst baseUrl = getBaseUrl(c);\n\nSame helper used by discovery.ts, x402.ts, and all other discovery routes.\n\n## Safety Gates\n- No changes to MCP JSON-RPC POST handler\n- No changes to tool definitions or auth\n- Verify: curl -s https://www.parsethis.ai/mcp shows https://\n- Verify /mcp.json unchanged\n- npm run typecheck and npm run build pass\n- Add test asserting https:// when x-forwarded-proto: https	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	elon:hourly_loop	elon:hourly_loop	2026-08-09 16:57:43.814	2026-08-09 16:57:43.814
cmsm3xwas0008ic1e7ax0h78t	elon-version-deploy-metadata-dockerfile-2026-08-09	Wire git commit SHA + build time into Docker build so /version is not blind	ops_reliability	8	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"runtime_mode": "source (runs tsx on src/index.ts, not dist/index.js — build runs tsc but CMD ignores dist)", "repo_head_sha": "82a63468695cf970d9ab1a62ea008908d96e53ac", "code_reads_from": ["PARSE_COMMIT_SHA", "RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA", "PARSE_BUILD_TIME", "RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_CREATED_AT"], "dockerfile_missing": "No ARG/ENV for commit SHA or build time. Railway Docker builds need explicit ARG passthrough.", "prod_version_response": {"commit": "unknown", "runtime": "source", "build_time": "unknown"}}	Unblocks every post-deploy verification pattern. Currently /version returns commit:unknown, build_time:unknown — there is zero deploy traceability. Makes rollback, deploy verification, and investor-readiness audits impossible. Fixing this is a one-Dockerfile-line change.	["Dockerfile accepts ARG RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA and ARG RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_CREATED_AT (or PARSE_COMMIT_SHA / PARSE_BUILD_TIME) and sets them as ENV at build time", "After deploy, GET https://www.parsethis.ai/version returns the actual commit SHA and build timestamp, not 'unknown'", "Deploy verification can diff prod /version commit against intended git HEAD", "Smoke test: curl /version | jq .deployment.commit is not 'unknown'", "Optional: CMD switches to node dist/index.js so runtime shows 'dist' not 'source'"]	Wire deploy metadata into Docker build (ARG/ENV for commit SHA + build time)	## Problem\nProduction /version returns commit:"unknown", build_time:"unknown" because the Dockerfile never passes Railway's build-time env vars (RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA, RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_CREATED_AT) into the image.\n\n## Fix\nAdd to Dockerfile before the build steps:\n```\nARG RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA\nARG RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_CREATED_AT\nENV PARSE_COMMIT_SHA=$RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA\nENV PARSE_BUILD_TIME=$RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_CREATED_AT\n```\nbuild-info.ts already reads these — no app code change needed.\n\n## Bonus\nCMD currently runs `tsx src/index.ts` despite building with tsc. Switch CMD to `node dist/index.js` so runtime="dist" and startup is faster. This is optional and lower priority.\n\n## Safety gates\n- Read-only change to Dockerfile only; no app logic changes\n- No billing, security, customer, or policy mutations\n- Test locally: docker build --build-arg RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA=test123 . → verify /version returns test123\n- Post-deploy: curl https://www.parsethis.ai/version | jq .deployment.commit must match git HEAD\n\n## Verification\n1. Local docker build with test commit SHA → /version returns it\n2. Deploy to Railway → /version commit matches deployed branch HEAD\n3. Runtime field changes to "dist" if CMD also updated	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Master Key:master	Master Key:master	2026-08-09 17:59:51.748	2026-08-09 17:59:51.748
cmsnv90el00ph9i1eyz340l0d	elon-free-tier-shared-10-rate-limit-bricks-agent-flows-2026-08-10	P0 FIRST-MILE: Free-tier shared rate_limit=10 bricks agent flows — /v1/parse 429 after 10 calls and also blocks DELETE /v1/keys/self + GET /v1/billing/usage	onboarding_first_mile	10	proposed	medium	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"host": "https://www.parsethis.ai", "keygen": {"http": 201, "note": "Self-service keys still land on redis_* storage; separate open card covers admin invisibility.", "id_prefix": "redis_", "scopes_returned": ["analyze", "evaluate", "chat"]}, "method": "Fresh POST /v1/keys/generate then sequential POST /v1/parse with safe prompts; then DELETE /v1/keys/self and GET /v1/billing/usage under the same bearer. No secrets printed.", "why_new": "Existing rate-limit cards ask for MORE keygen abuse friction or missing X-RateLimit headers. None cover free-tier shared bucket limit=10 bricking multi-step agent onboarding and blocking self-revoke/usage under the same counter.", "observed_at": "2026-08-10T23:32:05.870Z", "business_context": {"open_proposals": 90, "payments_total": 0, "approved_or_tasked": 0, "active_subscriptions": 0, "geo_30d_surface_hits": 1097, "api_keys_active_approx": 30, "geo_30d_unique_clients": 128, "screening_events_last_24h": 0}, "production_version": {"service": "Parse", "version": "1.0.0", "deployment": {"commit": "unknown", "runtime": "source", "build_time": "unknown"}}, "rate_matrix_safe_parse": {"calls_1_to_10": "HTTP 200 risk_score=0", "calls_11_to_15": "HTTP 429 code=rate_limit.exceeded limit=10 retry_after_seconds≈55-57", "limit_observed": 10}, "collateral_while_limited": {"DELETE /v1/keys/self": {"code": "rate_limit.exceeded", "http": 429, "limit": 10, "why_bad": "Customer cannot revoke a key they just burned quota on; security control shares the product RPM bucket."}, "GET /v1/billing/usage": {"code": "rate_limit.exceeded", "http": 429, "why_bad": "Agent cannot read remaining quota after hitting the wall; no X-RateLimit-* headers either (separate open card)."}}, "related_open_do_not_duplicate": ["keygen-abuse-ip-only-ratelimit-no-second-layer-2026-08-02", "keygen-rate-limit-abuse-protection-2026-07-30", "elon-x-ratelimit-headers-missing-2026-08-08", "elon-redis-keys-self-revoke-broken-2026-08-10", "elon-free-tier-usage-api-lies-zero-included-zero-counter-2026-08-08", "key-self-introspection-get-endpoint-2026-08-02"]}	Free self-service keys hit a shared 10-request ceiling so hard that a normal agent dogfood (parse smokes + usage check) gets 429 inside one minute. Worse: the same bucket blocks key revoke and usage reads, so customers cannot recover or measure quota after the wall. With discovery traffic and zero paid conversions, this is a silent first-mile kill — not optional polish.	["Fresh free self-service key can complete a documented first-mile matrix without 429: at least keygen + 5x /v1/parse + 1x /v1/screen-output + 1x /v1/billing/usage + 1x policy/read in one minute, OR docs explicitly publish the lower ceiling with Retry-After + X-RateLimit-* headers.", "DELETE /v1/keys/self is NOT charged against the same free product RPM bucket as /v1/parse (or is allowlisted with a separate higher security bucket) so a limited key can still be revoked.", "GET /v1/billing/usage remains available under rate limit pressure (separate bucket or exempt) so agents can read remaining quota after a 429.", "429 responses on free tier include machine-readable limit, remaining, reset/retry_after, and which bucket was exhausted (product vs auth vs keygen).", "Default free rate_limit is either raised to a dogfood-safe floor (recommend >=60/min product RPM or token-bucket burst) OR product copy/OpenAPI/llms.txt state the 10/min ceiling before install.", "Regression test: scripted free-key matrix asserts no 429 before the documented ceiling; asserts self-delete and usage succeed immediately after product 429.", "No billing/price/security-policy/global WAF changes in this task beyond rate-limit bucket split + free default + headers/docs. No live Stripe mutations."]	Split free-tier rate-limit buckets + raise/document ceiling so product 429s never block self-revoke or usage reads	## Problem\nLive production probe 2026-08-10 on https://www.parsethis.ai:\n\n1. POST /v1/keys/generate -> 201 redis_* free key, rate_limit effectively 10.\n2. POST /v1/parse safe prompts 1..10 -> 200.\n3. Call 11+ -> 429 rate_limit.exceeded limit=10 retry_after~55s.\n4. While limited, DELETE /v1/keys/self -> 429 (same bucket).\n5. While limited, GET /v1/billing/usage -> 429 (same bucket).\n\nThis is not "abuse protection on keygen". It is a shared free-tier product counter so low that a normal agent install + smoke + usage check dies mid-flow, and the customer cannot revoke the key or read quota after the wall.\n\nDiscovery traffic is real (~1097 surface hits / 128 clients / 30d) with 0 paid subs. First-mile friction at 10 shared RPM is a conversion + trust killer.\n\n## Goal\nMake free-tier rate limiting survivable for agent onboarding and keep security/quota endpoints usable under product pressure.\n\n## Implementation sketch\n1. Inspect rate-limit middleware / per-key counter (likely Redis) and identify single shared bucket across all bearer routes.\n2. Split buckets:\n   - product: /v1/parse, /v1/screen-output, /v1/analyze, /v1/chat, /v1/evaluate, /v1/agent/*\n   - account: /v1/billing/usage, /v1/policy, /v1/keys/self\n   - auth/keygen: separate existing limits\n3. Raise free product default to a dogfood-safe floor OR document 10/min everywhere if intentionally low.\n4. Emit X-RateLimit-Limit/Remaining/Reset + Retry-After on 429 and success where cheap.\n5. Tests for matrix + self-delete/usage exemption.\n6. Do not touch Stripe prices, Cloudflare Bot Fight, or global security policy in this card.\n\n## Safety gates\n- Read-only diagnosis first; mutate rate-limit code + defaults + docs/tests only.\n- No payment refunds/cancellations, no secret rotation, no hard deletes of customer data.\n- No weakening auth on admin routes.\n- Deploy only after typecheck/tests; verify on production with a disposable free key matrix.\n- If redis_* key storage still broken for revoke, link existing revoke card — this card still must not 429 account routes.\n\n## Acceptance\nSee acceptance_criteria on the proposal.	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Master Key:master	Master Key:master	2026-08-10 23:32:06.093	2026-08-10 23:32:06.093
cmsm8oow5001lvi1evqpp92fj	elon-sign-in-points-to-admin-login-not-user-login-2026-08-09	P0: Every "Sign in" link points to /admin/login (API-key dashboard) — the working user account system (/login, /signup, /account) is completely orphaned	conversion_friction	9	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"finding": "All Sign in links site-wide point to /admin/login instead of /login", "fix_scope": "Two href changes from /admin/login to /login", "verification": {"signup_works": "POST /auth/signup returns {ok:true, redirect:/account}", "user_login_auth": "Email/password based (POST /auth/login accepts {email, password})", "account_page_has": ["Subscription management", "API key create/revoke", "Billing", "Compliance"], "admin_login_auth": "API key based (POST /admin/login accepts {api_key})", "landing_signin_href": "/admin/login"}, "root_cause_locations": ["src/lib/html-template.ts:649 — shared header", "src/pages/landing.ts:464 — standalone header"]}	Conversion-critical: 100% of users who want to self-serve account management are sent to the wrong auth surface.	["All Sign in links point to /login", "Landing page header Sign in points to /login", "Shared html-template.ts header Sign in points to /login", "Navigating from landing to Sign in reaches email/password login form", "After login user reaches /account showing API keys and subscription", "Admin login /admin/login remains accessible but not default user path", "npm run typecheck passes", "npm run build passes"]	Fix Sign-in routing: point to /login instead of /admin/login	## Problem\nEvery Sign in link points to /admin/login (API-key admin auth) while a user account system exists at /login + /signup + /account.\n\n## Root Cause\n1. src/lib/html-template.ts:649 — shared header\n2. src/pages/landing.ts:464 — landing header\n\n## Fix\n1. Change href=/admin/login to href=/login in both files\n2. Add Sign up link to /signup\n3. Keep /admin/login accessible for dashboard cookie auth\n\n## Safety Gates\n- No auth/security/billing changes\n- Only navigation href changes\n- Test: typecheck + build	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Master Key:master	Master Key:master	2026-08-09 20:12:40.324	2026-08-09 20:12:40.324
cmsmaxgkz001uvi1e0g8ahbsi	elon-deploy-metadata-blindness-2026-08-09	Production /version and /health show commit=unknown, build_time=unknown — zero deploy traceability	ops_reliability	7	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"probe_date": "2026-08-09", "railway_config": "railway.toml builder=DOCKERFILE, startCommand=npx tsx src/index.ts", "health_endpoint": "GET https://www.parsethis.ai/health → commit:'unknown'", "version_endpoint": "GET https://www.parsethis.ai/version → commit:'unknown'", "build_info_source": "src/lib/build-info.ts reads process.env.RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA, RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_CREATED_AT", "root_cause_hypothesis": "Dockerfile runs npx tsc but startCommand uses tsx src/index.ts (not dist/), so runtime='source' is correct but commit SHA is not injected because Railway Docker builder does not pass RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA into the container env unless explicitly wired"}	Operators cannot verify which commit is live in production. Every deploy-gate, rollback verification, and incident diagnosis becomes guesswork. The existing code in src/lib/build-info.ts reads RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA and RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_CREATED_AT, but production returns 'unknown' for both, meaning Railway build env vars are not reaching the container at runtime. This is a first-order SaaS reliability gap.	["GET /version returns commit != 'unknown' after next Railway deploy", "GET /health returns commit != 'unknown' and build_time != 'unknown'", "Commit SHA in /version matches the git HEAD deployed", "Existing tests in src/app.test.ts that assert commit='unknown' are updated to assert non-unknown when RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA is present in test env"]	Wire Railway deploy metadata (commit SHA, build time) into production /version and /health	## Problem\nProduction /version and /health both return commit='unknown' and build_time='unknown'. The code in src/lib/build-info.ts already reads RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA and RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_CREATED_AT, but Railway's Docker builder does not inject these env vars into the container at runtime by default.\n\n## Root Cause\nrailway.toml uses builder=DOCKERFILE. The Dockerfile builds with tsc but runs via 'npx tsx src/index.ts'. Railway injects RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA and RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_CREATED_AT as build-time env vars, but the Dockerfile does not capture them as ARG/ENV. They are available during `docker build` but not baked into the image layers.\n\n## Fix Options (pick one)\n1. **Dockerfile ARG/ENV (recommended):** Add `ARG RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA` and `ARG RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_CREATED_AT` in Dockerfile, then `ENV PARSE_COMMIT_SHA=$RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA` and `ENV PARSE_BUILD_TIME=$RAILWAY_DEPLOYMENT_CREATED_AT` so they persist into runtime.\n2. **Build-time stamp file:** During docker build, write commit+timestamp to a JSON file (e.g. /app/build-info.json) and read it at startup.\n3. **Railway variable reference:** In Railway dashboard, set a runtime variable PARSE_COMMIT_SHA referencing $RAILWAY_GIT_COMMIT_SHA so it resolves at deploy time.\n\n## Safety Gates\n- Do NOT change startCommand or the tsx-vs-dist runtime decision — Prisma 7 ESM requires tsx.\n- Do NOT add secrets to the image.\n- Verify the commit SHA shown in /version is NOT a secret — it is a public git SHA.\n- Run npm run typecheck and npm run build after any change.\n- Update src/app.test.ts assertions that currently hardcode commit='unknown'.\n- After deploy, verify /version returns real commit matching git HEAD.\n\n## Verification\n- curl https://www.parsethis.ai/version | jq .deployment.commit (should be non-unknown)\n- curl https://www.parsethis.ai/health | jq .deployment.commit (should be non-unknown)	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	Master Key:master	Master Key:master	2026-08-09 21:15:28.691	2026-08-09 21:15:28.691
cmsmd7xdf00007o1evzak0fjr	elon-x402-discovery-mismatch-2026-08-09	x402 payment path advertised across all agent discovery surfaces but disabled in production	saas_readiness	8	proposed	low	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	{"probes": {"/llms.txt": {"advertises_x402": true}, "/mcp.json": {"advertises_x402": true}, "/v1/pricing": {"payTo": "not_configured", "enabled": false}, "/openapi.json": {"advertises_402": true}, "/v1/parse_no_auth": {"http_status": 401, "expected_if_x402": 402}}, "root_cause": "X402_ENABLED env not set and/or X402_PAY_TO_ADDRESS missing in production. src/x402.ts lines 18-19,44-46,445.", "collected_at": "2026-08-09T22:17:00Z", "business_impact": "Autonomous agents following discovery docs get 401 instead of advertised 402, blocking zero-friction onboarding."}	Blocks zero-friction agent onboarding. Agents discovering Parse via llms.txt/OpenAPI/MCP that attempt x402 pay-per-call get 401 instead of the advertised 402 payment flow.	["POST /v1/parse with no auth returns HTTP 402 with valid x402 accepts[] when X402 enabled", "GET /v1/pricing returns enabled: true with valid payTo wallet", "Decision: either enable x402 with configured wallet, or remove x402 claims from discovery surfaces until ready"]	Resolve x402 payment discovery mismatch: enable x402 in production or remove x402 claims from agent discovery surfaces	Production /v1/pricing returns enabled:false but all four agent discovery surfaces (/llms.txt, /openapi.json, /mcp.json, /mcp) advertise x402. Agents get 401 instead of 402.\n\nOption A: Set X402_ENABLED=true and X402_PAY_TO_ADDRESS in Railway prod env. Smoke 402->pay->retry.\nOption B: Remove x402 refs from llms.txt/openapi/mcp.json until ready.\n\nSafety: no billing/pricing/security changes. Option A needs wallet approval from Danny. Option B is docs-only. Run typecheck+build+tests, verify /version, post-deploy smoke.	triage	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	elon_hourly_saas_improvement_loop	2026-08-09 22:19:36.243	2026